WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:06.120 --> 00:00:08.850
- Welcome, everybody.

00:00:08.850 --> 00:00:11.490
I'm Maurice Samuels, the director

00:00:11.490 --> 00:00:14.700
of the Yale Program for
the Study of Anti-Semitism,

00:00:14.700 --> 00:00:17.340
and it's my great pleasure
to welcome you today

00:00:17.340 --> 00:00:20.460
to this talk by Richard Hurowitz.

00:00:20.460 --> 00:00:23.370
Richard is a writer, investor,

00:00:23.370 --> 00:00:26.490
and the publisher of
the "Octavian Report,"

00:00:26.490 --> 00:00:27.870
a quarterly magazine

00:00:27.870 --> 00:00:31.320
devoted to politics,
economics, and culture.

00:00:31.320 --> 00:00:34.260
His writing has appeared
in "The New York Times,"

00:00:34.260 --> 00:00:36.907
the "Financial Times,"
"The Wall Street Journal,"

00:00:36.907 --> 00:00:40.140
"The Times" of London,
"The Jerusalem Post,"

00:00:40.140 --> 00:00:42.450
and many other publications.

00:00:42.450 --> 00:00:45.217
His new book, "In the
Garden of the Righteous:

00:00:45.217 --> 00:00:47.377
"The Heroes Who Risked Their Lives

00:00:47.377 --> 00:00:49.920
"to Save Jews During the Holocaust,"

00:00:49.920 --> 00:00:53.580
was published just last
week by HarperCollins

00:00:53.580 --> 00:00:57.180
and has received a lot of attention.

00:00:57.180 --> 00:00:59.857
The "Kirkus" starred review calls it,

00:00:59.857 --> 00:01:02.467
"A fresh, engrossing contribution

00:01:02.467 --> 00:01:04.417
"to the literature of the Holocaust,

00:01:04.417 --> 00:01:08.940
"focusing on heroics rather than despair."

00:01:08.940 --> 00:01:11.490
Richard received his BA in history

00:01:11.490 --> 00:01:14.640
from Yale in 1995, magna cum laude,

00:01:14.640 --> 00:01:17.400
and was elected to Phi Beta Kappa.

00:01:17.400 --> 00:01:20.040
He earned his JD from Columbia Law School,

00:01:20.040 --> 00:01:23.580
where he was a Harlan Fiske Stone Scholar.

00:01:23.580 --> 00:01:26.010
He lives in New York City with his family,

00:01:26.010 --> 00:01:29.340
and his son Asher is
currently a first year at Yale

00:01:29.340 --> 00:01:30.783
in Berkeley College,

00:01:31.800 --> 00:01:34.350
so here's how it's gonna work today.

00:01:34.350 --> 00:01:37.470
Richard will speak for
about 30 or 40 minutes.

00:01:37.470 --> 00:01:40.380
Then we'll open it up to questions.

00:01:40.380 --> 00:01:43.020
Please submit your questions to me

00:01:43.020 --> 00:01:47.100
using the Q&amp;A function at
the bottom of your screen.

00:01:47.100 --> 00:01:50.520
You can submit the questions
at any time during the lecture,

00:01:50.520 --> 00:01:54.420
and I'll ask them to Richard
during the Q&amp;A period.

00:01:54.420 --> 00:01:56.460
We can't actually see or hear you,

00:01:56.460 --> 00:01:59.490
so that's the only way
to communicate with us,

00:01:59.490 --> 00:02:02.910
and we'll wrap up at 6:00 p.m.,

00:02:02.910 --> 00:02:06.960
so now I will hand it over to Richard.

00:02:06.960 --> 00:02:08.250
- Thank you. Well, thank you, Maury.

00:02:08.250 --> 00:02:09.840
Thank you, Vanessa,

00:02:09.840 --> 00:02:12.900
and it's a really great pleasure

00:02:12.900 --> 00:02:14.433
and very special to be here.

00:02:15.420 --> 00:02:18.570
Yale is a very, very special place for me,

00:02:18.570 --> 00:02:20.163
an important part of my life,

00:02:21.300 --> 00:02:22.920
and as I'll talk in a moment,

00:02:22.920 --> 00:02:24.360
kind of where this book started,

00:02:24.360 --> 00:02:28.350
and it remains a special part of my life,

00:02:28.350 --> 00:02:33.030
and hopefully, I will be up
there later in the semester.

00:02:33.030 --> 00:02:33.863
As Maury mentioned,

00:02:33.863 --> 00:02:35.370
I have a personal reason for being there,

00:02:35.370 --> 00:02:36.990
which is my son just started,

00:02:36.990 --> 00:02:40.170
and is doing far more interesting things

00:02:40.170 --> 00:02:41.790
than I ever did there,

00:02:41.790 --> 00:02:44.070
but anyway, thank you all

00:02:44.070 --> 00:02:46.800
for joining this conversation,

00:02:46.800 --> 00:02:50.310
and I'm a big admirer of, Maury, your work

00:02:50.310 --> 00:02:53.010
and the Yale Program for
the Study of Anti-Semitism,

00:02:53.010 --> 00:02:56.730
which I believe is the only such program

00:02:56.730 --> 00:02:57.570
in the United States,

00:02:57.570 --> 00:03:01.233
so it's really important
work that's being done there,

00:03:02.160 --> 00:03:04.650
so let me see.

00:03:04.650 --> 00:03:09.570
There, okay, so this is
the origin of the book.

00:03:09.570 --> 00:03:13.290
On the left is something
you'll all recognize,

00:03:13.290 --> 00:03:15.543
which is Sterling,

00:03:16.380 --> 00:03:17.940
and on the right is the Holocaust Museum.

00:03:17.940 --> 00:03:18.930
I put these images up

00:03:18.930 --> 00:03:22.740
because the Holocaust Museum on the mall

00:03:22.740 --> 00:03:25.890
opened its doors when I was at Yale,

00:03:25.890 --> 00:03:29.940
and my family has been in this country

00:03:29.940 --> 00:03:33.450
for many generations, so
predating the Holocaust,

00:03:33.450 --> 00:03:36.060
although my wife's family,

00:03:36.060 --> 00:03:38.560
my mother-in-law actually
is a Holocaust survivor,

00:03:39.630 --> 00:03:41.910
but we, like a lot of American Jews,

00:03:41.910 --> 00:03:45.810
made the pilgrimage down
to the museum on the right,

00:03:45.810 --> 00:03:49.380
and anybody who's ever
been through the museum

00:03:49.380 --> 00:03:53.100
knows that it's an
extraordinarily difficult,

00:03:53.100 --> 00:03:57.630
enervating experience, intentionally so.

00:03:57.630 --> 00:04:01.083
The artifacts are very difficult to see.

00:04:03.690 --> 00:04:04.523
I remember,

00:04:04.523 --> 00:04:06.840
and as I've been discussing
the book with a lot of people,

00:04:06.840 --> 00:04:09.390
this is something that
seems to have a huge impact

00:04:09.390 --> 00:04:10.380
on anyone who sees it,

00:04:10.380 --> 00:04:13.000
which is this pile of shoes that is there

00:04:14.073 --> 00:04:16.980
from, you know, victims of the Shoah.

00:04:16.980 --> 00:04:18.300
Many of them, when you look closely,

00:04:18.300 --> 00:04:19.500
are in children's sizes,

00:04:19.500 --> 00:04:20.790
so we always have to remember

00:04:20.790 --> 00:04:23.203
that of the 6 million who died,

00:04:23.203 --> 00:04:25.230
1 1/2 million were children,

00:04:25.230 --> 00:04:29.490
and anyway, for me, it
was an extraordinarily,

00:04:29.490 --> 00:04:33.390
almost overwhelming, very
upsetting experience,

00:04:33.390 --> 00:04:36.990
and I came out into a room that was there

00:04:36.990 --> 00:04:41.040
that was dedicated to the
very small number of people

00:04:41.040 --> 00:04:44.910
who tried to do something
to stop the Holocaust,

00:04:44.910 --> 00:04:48.063
to stop Nazism, to stop totalitarianism,

00:04:48.063 --> 00:04:50.700
and immediately saw these pictures,

00:04:50.700 --> 00:04:53.103
and particularly the one on the left,

00:04:53.940 --> 00:04:58.620
and this young man, Alexander Schmorell,

00:04:58.620 --> 00:05:00.990
for some reason, this picture
stuck with me at the time.

00:05:00.990 --> 00:05:02.490
I still remember seeing it.

00:05:02.490 --> 00:05:07.350
He was, I guess, around the
same age I was at the time,

00:05:07.350 --> 00:05:10.230
and he was a medical student,

00:05:10.230 --> 00:05:12.480
and he was part of something
called the White Rose,

00:05:12.480 --> 00:05:15.690
which was a small group of
students that was started by,

00:05:15.690 --> 00:05:19.590
on the right, you see,
actually four of them.

00:05:19.590 --> 00:05:22.020
He's actually, his face is blocked,

00:05:22.020 --> 00:05:24.960
but the girl in the
middle is Sophie Scholl,

00:05:24.960 --> 00:05:27.210
and she was an undergraduate
at the University of Munich.

00:05:27.210 --> 00:05:28.710
Her brother, on the left, Hans Scholl,

00:05:28.710 --> 00:05:33.710
was really the founder of the movement,

00:05:33.840 --> 00:05:38.250
and they were very idealistic students.

00:05:38.250 --> 00:05:41.433
The Scholls came from
a very unusual family.

00:05:42.338 --> 00:05:44.790
They were from the same
town as Einstein, Ulm.

00:05:44.790 --> 00:05:48.120
Their father was an ardent
believer in liberal democracy,

00:05:48.120 --> 00:05:52.680
but they were also devout Christians,

00:05:52.680 --> 00:05:55.890
and he believed in freedom

00:05:55.890 --> 00:05:57.450
and told them that, you know,

00:05:57.450 --> 00:05:58.620
they should do whatever they can

00:05:58.620 --> 00:06:01.603
to stand up for other people.

00:06:01.603 --> 00:06:02.436
I had the privilege

00:06:02.436 --> 00:06:05.520
of meeting her best
friend, who was Jewish,

00:06:05.520 --> 00:06:08.820
who told me, you know,
all about, you know,

00:06:08.820 --> 00:06:11.250
the kind of idealism of the family.

00:06:11.250 --> 00:06:12.960
They were also notable,

00:06:12.960 --> 00:06:13.920
so what they started doing

00:06:13.920 --> 00:06:16.620
was circulating leaflets
to other students,

00:06:16.620 --> 00:06:17.880
but also to just, you know,

00:06:17.880 --> 00:06:20.610
they would put them in, and
mail them to random people

00:06:20.610 --> 00:06:23.430
around Munich and throughout Germany

00:06:23.430 --> 00:06:27.960
talking about that this is
not the Germany we know.

00:06:27.960 --> 00:06:30.330
They quoted Plato. They quoted Aristotle.

00:06:30.330 --> 00:06:32.400
They quoted Chinese philosophers,

00:06:32.400 --> 00:06:36.390
and they tried to foment
a uprising against Hitler.

00:06:36.390 --> 00:06:37.830
This went on for a year.

00:06:37.830 --> 00:06:41.820
They put graffiti in Munich,

00:06:41.820 --> 00:06:45.240
and Schmorell, the guy with the pipe,

00:06:45.240 --> 00:06:46.800
he actually is among the first people

00:06:46.800 --> 00:06:48.060
to publicize the Holocaust.

00:06:48.060 --> 00:06:50.247
In the second leaflet, he
wrote that at that point,

00:06:50.247 --> 00:06:54.450
300,000 Jews had been killed
in the most bestial way,

00:06:54.450 --> 00:06:56.400
so these were just, like,

00:06:56.400 --> 00:07:00.723
wonderful people, young people,

00:07:01.831 --> 00:07:03.540
and then, unfortunately, one day,

00:07:03.540 --> 00:07:06.870
they were handing leaflets
out in the university,

00:07:06.870 --> 00:07:08.580
and they came to the top of a staircase,

00:07:08.580 --> 00:07:11.670
and Sophie realized that she
had a lot of extra leaflets,

00:07:11.670 --> 00:07:14.730
and there was an atrium, and
she threw them in the air.

00:07:14.730 --> 00:07:15.960
They started floating down.

00:07:15.960 --> 00:07:17.850
A maintenance man, who was an ardent Nazi,

00:07:17.850 --> 00:07:19.923
saw them, locked the door.

00:07:20.790 --> 00:07:22.320
They were arrested.

00:07:22.320 --> 00:07:25.260
Hitler personally sent his judge

00:07:25.260 --> 00:07:27.240
down from Berlin.

00:07:27.240 --> 00:07:29.583
They were put on trial,

00:07:30.777 --> 00:07:33.210
and they were executed,

00:07:33.210 --> 00:07:37.350
and right before Hans
was executed, you know,

00:07:37.350 --> 00:07:39.997
he shouted out in the,
it was very famous now,

00:07:39.997 --> 00:07:41.940
"Long live freedom!"

00:07:41.940 --> 00:07:46.410
And Sophie, when she was on trial,

00:07:46.410 --> 00:07:47.730
made a comment.

00:07:47.730 --> 00:07:50.527
She said, "Everyone
believes what we believe,

00:07:50.527 --> 00:07:52.710
"but everyone is afraid to say it,"

00:07:52.710 --> 00:07:56.430
and unfortunately, that was not
the case, as we'll get into.

00:07:56.430 --> 00:07:57.390
They were very (indistinct).

00:07:57.390 --> 00:07:59.220
There are many, many schools in Germany

00:07:59.220 --> 00:08:01.440
that were very well known in Germany,

00:08:01.440 --> 00:08:02.850
not so much outside of Germany,

00:08:02.850 --> 00:08:04.830
because people want to believe, you know,

00:08:04.830 --> 00:08:06.240
this is how everyone behaved,

00:08:06.240 --> 00:08:08.220
but it was very, very rare,

00:08:08.220 --> 00:08:10.860
but to me, it was also, at that time,

00:08:10.860 --> 00:08:13.020
a bit of a balm and an antidote

00:08:13.020 --> 00:08:16.830
to the horror of what I had
experienced at the museum,

00:08:16.830 --> 00:08:18.000
and the story stayed with me,

00:08:18.000 --> 00:08:20.250
and, you know, I was a
history major at Yale,

00:08:20.250 --> 00:08:23.365
and I actually thought about
writing my senior essay

00:08:23.365 --> 00:08:24.690
about the White Rose,

00:08:24.690 --> 00:08:26.580
but I realized I didn't know German,

00:08:26.580 --> 00:08:28.200
and I had a great experience.

00:08:28.200 --> 00:08:30.960
I wrote with Donald Kagan
on Alexander the Great,

00:08:30.960 --> 00:08:33.060
but the story stayed with me,

00:08:33.060 --> 00:08:36.580
and I ended up writing about it

00:08:37.740 --> 00:08:39.180
a number of years ago,

00:08:39.180 --> 00:08:40.680
but, you know, about five years ago

00:08:40.680 --> 00:08:42.300
for "The New York Times,"

00:08:42.300 --> 00:08:45.330
I wrote on the 75th
anniversary of their execution,

00:08:45.330 --> 00:08:46.320
like, sort of memorial,

00:08:46.320 --> 00:08:47.880
and it's telling their story

00:08:47.880 --> 00:08:50.190
and saying that we
should really honor them,

00:08:50.190 --> 00:08:51.360
and the response that I had

00:08:51.360 --> 00:08:55.290
was just so enormous and so positive,

00:08:55.290 --> 00:08:57.690
and as you all know, the
Internet is a very scary place.

00:08:57.690 --> 00:09:00.540
There was not one negative
comment about them,

00:09:00.540 --> 00:09:04.590
so I started writing up
stories of the righteous,

00:09:04.590 --> 00:09:06.630
and I had the same experience

00:09:06.630 --> 00:09:08.190
with a number of articles I wrote

00:09:08.190 --> 00:09:10.020
in "The New York Times" and "The Journal"

00:09:10.020 --> 00:09:11.880
and the "L.A. Times,"
and "The Daily Beast,"

00:09:11.880 --> 00:09:14.313
and so I decided to turn them into a book.

00:09:16.698 --> 00:09:19.890
The thing about it is that rescue

00:09:19.890 --> 00:09:24.210
is a very under-covered,

00:09:24.210 --> 00:09:28.830
under-focused-on area
for a variety of reasons,

00:09:28.830 --> 00:09:29.820
and it's unusual

00:09:29.820 --> 00:09:32.965
because people really have
seemed to respond to the stories.

00:09:32.965 --> 00:09:34.140
I mean, it's not just my experience,

00:09:34.140 --> 00:09:35.910
but, like, there was a movie
called "Schindler's List"

00:09:35.910 --> 00:09:39.273
that did pretty well, both
critically and commercially,

00:09:40.140 --> 00:09:41.517
so right after the war,

00:09:41.517 --> 00:09:44.940
and I have here another Yale image.

00:09:44.940 --> 00:09:46.620
This is a picture of
the Milgram experiments.

00:09:46.620 --> 00:09:47.970
You know, after the war,

00:09:47.970 --> 00:09:52.200
people were very interested
in the evil that happened.

00:09:52.200 --> 00:09:55.830
They were interested in
why people collaborated.

00:09:55.830 --> 00:09:57.240
They were not so interested

00:09:57.240 --> 00:10:00.600
in those small number
of people who helped,

00:10:00.600 --> 00:10:02.340
and there was also a view,

00:10:02.340 --> 00:10:04.590
in the first generation
of survivors, you know,

00:10:04.590 --> 00:10:06.780
many of whom actually were
not rescued and had gone,

00:10:06.780 --> 00:10:09.900
had horrible experiences
in concentration camps,

00:10:09.900 --> 00:10:12.600
that you not distort the record

00:10:12.600 --> 00:10:15.090
by focusing on something that was so rare.

00:10:15.090 --> 00:10:17.010
I put a statistic up here,

00:10:17.010 --> 00:10:18.600
so Yad Vashem in Jerusalem,

00:10:18.600 --> 00:10:20.220
and their criterion are very stringent,

00:10:20.220 --> 00:10:24.810
has recognized order of
magnitude 27,000 people

00:10:24.810 --> 00:10:28.320
with the title Righteous
Among the Nations,

00:10:28.320 --> 00:10:29.550
which seems like a large number,

00:10:29.550 --> 00:10:32.460
but that's out of 500 million people

00:10:32.460 --> 00:10:33.900
that were in Europe at the time,

00:10:33.900 --> 00:10:35.940
so even if you multiply by 10,

00:10:35.940 --> 00:10:38.430
it's still a vanishingly
small number of people.

00:10:38.430 --> 00:10:40.740
If you were to fill Madison Square Garden

00:10:40.740 --> 00:10:43.569
with a random number of the 500 million,

00:10:43.569 --> 00:10:46.710
you'd have approximately
one rescuer there,

00:10:46.710 --> 00:10:50.763
so unfortunately, you know,

00:10:51.720 --> 00:10:54.690
people always knew Raoul
Wallenberg, you know,

00:10:54.690 --> 00:10:57.480
and then, after the movie,
they know Oskar Schindler.

00:10:57.480 --> 00:11:00.360
but there's been very
little attention paid

00:11:00.360 --> 00:11:02.400
to any of the other rescuers.

00:11:02.400 --> 00:11:05.040
Some people know the story of Denmark,

00:11:05.040 --> 00:11:07.920
and the next generation of survivors,

00:11:07.920 --> 00:11:08.997
one of the leaders of which

00:11:08.997 --> 00:11:10.500
was a friend of mine, Abe Foxman,

00:11:10.500 --> 00:11:13.470
who was the longtime head of the ADL,

00:11:13.470 --> 00:11:14.770
had more of an interest

00:11:16.735 --> 00:11:18.900
in focusing on righteousness,

00:11:18.900 --> 00:11:21.120
and the Righteous Among
the Nations program

00:11:21.120 --> 00:11:23.553
has been part of Yad
Vashem from its founding.

00:11:24.690 --> 00:11:26.160
It's one of its core missions.

00:11:26.160 --> 00:11:28.140
Just to pause for a moment,

00:11:28.140 --> 00:11:31.110
the title of my book "In the
Garden of the Righteous,"

00:11:31.110 --> 00:11:33.210
is actually a real place in Jerusalem,

00:11:33.210 --> 00:11:35.370
and the reason, what it is,

00:11:35.370 --> 00:11:38.610
is when someone is
recognized as righteous,

00:11:38.610 --> 00:11:39.730
they plant a tree

00:11:42.197 --> 00:11:44.310
in the garden there,

00:11:44.310 --> 00:11:45.630
which is now almost a forest,

00:11:45.630 --> 00:11:47.880
and it's a very powerful
experience to walk through it,

00:11:47.880 --> 00:11:52.140
and, you know, it's a
very beautiful sort of

00:11:52.140 --> 00:11:56.313
and very poetic place
and gesture that's made.

00:11:57.150 --> 00:12:01.440
You know, for me, I think
there's a handful of reasons

00:12:01.440 --> 00:12:05.940
that it's very important to
actually focus on rescue,

00:12:05.940 --> 00:12:08.130
and there was the purpose of the book,

00:12:08.130 --> 00:12:09.180
and I tried to talk about this

00:12:09.180 --> 00:12:12.150
in my introduction and conclusion.

00:12:12.150 --> 00:12:13.620
One is out of gratitude

00:12:13.620 --> 00:12:16.200
because I think there's,

00:12:16.200 --> 00:12:18.780
it's very important that, you know,

00:12:18.780 --> 00:12:21.900
it's both a Jewish value
and a humanist value

00:12:21.900 --> 00:12:24.390
to say thank you to people

00:12:24.390 --> 00:12:26.910
who did among the greatest acts, I think,

00:12:26.910 --> 00:12:29.220
in the history of humanity, truly,

00:12:29.220 --> 00:12:32.250
and risked their lives, often
for people they didn't know,

00:12:32.250 --> 00:12:34.620
sometimes for people
they didn't even like,

00:12:34.620 --> 00:12:36.720
and just were unbelievably heroic

00:12:36.720 --> 00:12:39.210
at probably the worst moment in history,

00:12:39.210 --> 00:12:42.510
and a rabbi, who was one of the founders

00:12:42.510 --> 00:12:44.610
of the movement to recognize the righteous

00:12:44.610 --> 00:12:46.687
also said, "There's a historical injustice

00:12:46.687 --> 00:12:47.557
"that we all know the names

00:12:47.557 --> 00:12:50.377
"of Himmler and Goering and Hitler,

00:12:50.377 --> 00:12:51.307
"but we don't know the names

00:12:51.307 --> 00:12:53.850
"of people like Sousa Mendes or Sugihara,"

00:12:53.850 --> 00:12:55.800
and so it's an attempt to correct that.

00:12:57.120 --> 00:13:00.180
I also think there are enormous
lessons that we can learn

00:13:00.180 --> 00:13:04.080
from why people did
this, how they did this.

00:13:04.080 --> 00:13:05.670
Since the Second World War,

00:13:05.670 --> 00:13:07.890
there have been more than 40 genocides.

00:13:07.890 --> 00:13:10.600
We're living through a moment of horrific

00:13:13.710 --> 00:13:16.980
war crimes being committed
in more than one place.

00:13:16.980 --> 00:13:18.240
I mean, obviously, in Ukraine,

00:13:18.240 --> 00:13:20.847
you have a million Uighurs
in concentration camps.

00:13:20.847 --> 00:13:24.450
500,000 people lost their
lives in the Syrian civil war,

00:13:24.450 --> 00:13:25.857
and there's also goodness
going on right now,

00:13:25.857 --> 00:13:27.480
and I think we need to learn that,

00:13:27.480 --> 00:13:31.110
and the lessons also are
relevant for our own society

00:13:31.110 --> 00:13:33.120
to try to make it a better place,

00:13:33.120 --> 00:13:37.140
and certainly, to try to avoid
anything like the Holocaust

00:13:37.140 --> 00:13:39.270
or the Second World War
from happening again,

00:13:39.270 --> 00:13:41.040
and then, I think there's also,

00:13:41.040 --> 00:13:43.290
it is important, not just for children,

00:13:43.290 --> 00:13:46.620
but for anybody who had
the experience I had,

00:13:46.620 --> 00:13:49.120
that, you know, a friend of mine

00:13:51.150 --> 00:13:53.550
kind of called "The Righteous"

00:13:53.550 --> 00:13:56.460
a banister you could hold to
learn about the Holocaust,

00:13:56.460 --> 00:13:59.010
and so for many people, you know,

00:13:59.010 --> 00:14:03.450
when you start to talk about
6 million Jews dead, you know,

00:14:03.450 --> 00:14:05.880
hundreds of thousands
of other people killed,

00:14:05.880 --> 00:14:09.060
50 million people during
the Second World War,

00:14:09.060 --> 00:14:10.290
it's very difficult,

00:14:10.290 --> 00:14:12.780
and so a way of sort of, you know,

00:14:12.780 --> 00:14:15.360
beginning to think about
and understand the Holocaust

00:14:15.360 --> 00:14:17.280
and combat things like Holocaust denial

00:14:17.280 --> 00:14:19.230
is to talk about the good.

00:14:19.230 --> 00:14:23.040
I mean, I remember Mister
Rogers, after 9/11,

00:14:23.040 --> 00:14:25.020
they brought him out of retirement,

00:14:25.020 --> 00:14:25.957
and then he was asked,

00:14:25.957 --> 00:14:27.810
"How do we talk about
this with the children?"

00:14:27.810 --> 00:14:30.840
And he said, "We need
to look to the helpers,"

00:14:30.840 --> 00:14:33.660
and so I think that is important,

00:14:33.660 --> 00:14:35.640
and so that's another reason

00:14:35.640 --> 00:14:38.310
why I think these stories need to be told

00:14:38.310 --> 00:14:39.813
and need to be remembered,

00:14:41.790 --> 00:14:45.570
so, you know, one question,

00:14:45.570 --> 00:14:47.280
like, why did people do this?

00:14:47.280 --> 00:14:50.640
So there was some work done.

00:14:50.640 --> 00:14:54.540
This picture is another amazing
person. He's in my book.

00:14:54.540 --> 00:14:58.230
He was the cousin of the Trocmés,

00:14:58.230 --> 00:15:00.270
who were the pastor and his wife

00:15:00.270 --> 00:15:02.640
in a town called Le Chambon
I'll talk about in a moment,

00:15:02.640 --> 00:15:06.360
but he was a teacher in his early 30s.

00:15:06.360 --> 00:15:10.560
He taught both adolescents
and small children.

00:15:10.560 --> 00:15:12.780
He was, like, the equivalent of,

00:15:12.780 --> 00:15:17.780
like, the head of the college at Yale,

00:15:18.150 --> 00:15:20.640
and he gave, he lost his life

00:15:20.640 --> 00:15:22.650
'cause there was a,
like, a Gestapo roundup,

00:15:22.650 --> 00:15:25.950
and he was sent to a
concentration camp and gassed,

00:15:25.950 --> 00:15:29.820
but he was, he wrote home
even after he'd been arrested,

00:15:29.820 --> 00:15:32.340
but before that, he'd found his calling

00:15:32.340 --> 00:15:35.490
that helping people, helping
children, hide children

00:15:35.490 --> 00:15:38.100
was really what he was
put on this earth to do,

00:15:38.100 --> 00:15:40.263
so there was some study done.

00:15:41.160 --> 00:15:44.310
There was one pretty systematic study

00:15:44.310 --> 00:15:46.140
that was done by two Holocaust survivors,

00:15:46.140 --> 00:15:46.980
a husband and a wife,

00:15:46.980 --> 00:15:51.210
who were also psychologists, the Oliners.

00:15:51.210 --> 00:15:53.250
They wrote a book called
"The Altruistic Personality,"

00:15:53.250 --> 00:15:56.850
and they looked at all
different kinds of reasons

00:15:56.850 --> 00:15:58.200
people might have done this.

00:15:58.200 --> 00:16:00.630
It's not entirely satisfying.

00:16:00.630 --> 00:16:03.720
Their main conclusion, believe it or not,

00:16:03.720 --> 00:16:06.960
was that the only
correlation they really found

00:16:06.960 --> 00:16:09.210
was with parental discipline,

00:16:09.210 --> 00:16:13.710
so people who disciplined their children

00:16:13.710 --> 00:16:16.410
in a loving, rational way,

00:16:16.410 --> 00:16:18.990
who explained what the
child had done wrong,

00:16:18.990 --> 00:16:21.390
where the punishment was commensurate

00:16:21.390 --> 00:16:23.940
with what they did wrong,

00:16:23.940 --> 00:16:27.450
they tended to be more rescuers

00:16:27.450 --> 00:16:28.283
as opposed to people

00:16:28.283 --> 00:16:30.300
who developed more of an
authoritarian personality

00:16:30.300 --> 00:16:32.700
because their parents
would fly off the handle

00:16:32.700 --> 00:16:34.500
and corporal punishment and all of that,

00:16:34.500 --> 00:16:36.783
and I think my,

00:16:37.710 --> 00:16:40.110
just a few lessons that
I've seen from this

00:16:40.110 --> 00:16:42.960
that I think can build on that
is that there's no question

00:16:42.960 --> 00:16:45.600
that the single most important thing

00:16:45.600 --> 00:16:47.490
that made someone into a rescuer

00:16:47.490 --> 00:16:51.000
was having a strong parent role model

00:16:51.000 --> 00:16:52.020
or both parents

00:16:52.020 --> 00:16:55.350
or some other, you know,
figure in their life

00:16:55.350 --> 00:16:57.900
that taught them at a
very young age, you know,

00:16:57.900 --> 00:16:59.490
to be tolerant of others,

00:16:59.490 --> 00:17:02.253
that bigotry was wrong,
that bullying was wrong,

00:17:03.240 --> 00:17:06.690
and also, they tended to
come from very loving homes

00:17:06.690 --> 00:17:07.770
where they were valued,

00:17:07.770 --> 00:17:10.440
and this led to strong self-esteem,

00:17:10.440 --> 00:17:11.670
and that's very important

00:17:11.670 --> 00:17:14.430
because another correlation
is that most of the rescuers

00:17:14.430 --> 00:17:17.880
were people who had very
strong inner compass

00:17:17.880 --> 00:17:20.580
and were willing to listen to themselves

00:17:20.580 --> 00:17:23.670
when everything else was
kind of falling apart,

00:17:23.670 --> 00:17:27.120
so one lesson, for sure, is,

00:17:27.120 --> 00:17:30.990
like, how people grow up,
the home they grow up in

00:17:30.990 --> 00:17:33.990
has a huge impact on
the people they become

00:17:33.990 --> 00:17:37.410
and the values that they
have and their ability,

00:17:37.410 --> 00:17:38.880
their willingness to speak out.

00:17:38.880 --> 00:17:41.220
Educational attainment, I
say this to what's probably,

00:17:41.220 --> 00:17:43.170
what is definitely a
highly educated audience,

00:17:43.170 --> 00:17:44.463
completely noncorrelated.

00:17:45.480 --> 00:17:46.560
Most rescuers had a belief

00:17:46.560 --> 00:17:48.090
in something greater than themselves.

00:17:48.090 --> 00:17:51.393
The single most common
was religion of all sorts.

00:17:52.530 --> 00:17:53.363
Could have been something

00:17:53.363 --> 00:17:55.920
like liberal democracy or nonviolence.

00:17:55.920 --> 00:17:56.850
These were people, though,

00:17:56.850 --> 00:17:58.623
when I say religion of real faith,

00:17:59.828 --> 00:18:03.810
they really internalized
the teachings of, you know,

00:18:03.810 --> 00:18:05.730
love thy neighbor as thyself,

00:18:05.730 --> 00:18:07.230
the parable of the Good Samaritan.

00:18:07.230 --> 00:18:08.760
They were not people who
were interested in the church

00:18:08.760 --> 00:18:10.773
for signs of external piety.

00:18:12.000 --> 00:18:14.040
People who were in creative professions

00:18:14.040 --> 00:18:16.710
or who were in careers

00:18:16.710 --> 00:18:19.560
that brought them into
international, you know,

00:18:19.560 --> 00:18:21.870
a lot of contact with other
people all around the world

00:18:21.870 --> 00:18:24.630
also tended to be rescuers,

00:18:24.630 --> 00:18:26.250
and a couple of other things just to note

00:18:26.250 --> 00:18:27.360
that are kind of universal.

00:18:27.360 --> 00:18:28.860
They're almost universally modest.

00:18:28.860 --> 00:18:30.090
Over and over,

00:18:30.090 --> 00:18:32.340
and I've looked at hundreds if
not thousands of these cases,

00:18:32.340 --> 00:18:33.173
they would say things like,

00:18:33.173 --> 00:18:35.287
"I just did what any normal
person would've done.

00:18:35.287 --> 00:18:37.710
"I just did the decent thing,"

00:18:37.710 --> 00:18:40.170
and, of course, that's not
true, but that is sincere.

00:18:40.170 --> 00:18:42.570
I don't think there's
false modesty in that,

00:18:42.570 --> 00:18:43.920
and there's an unusual number of them

00:18:43.920 --> 00:18:45.960
that actually suffered for what they did.

00:18:45.960 --> 00:18:47.670
Oskar Schindler had rocks thrown at him

00:18:47.670 --> 00:18:49.623
in Germany after the war,

00:18:50.700 --> 00:18:53.100
and reasons for that,

00:18:53.100 --> 00:18:54.720
and I've talked to a lot
of experts about this,

00:18:54.720 --> 00:18:56.340
one was, you know,

00:18:56.340 --> 00:18:58.140
I mean, anti-Semitism
was a very real thing,

00:18:58.140 --> 00:19:01.620
and it didn't go away just
because the Nazis lost the war.

00:19:01.620 --> 00:19:03.420
People also questioned people for,

00:19:03.420 --> 00:19:05.820
like, bringing potential
risk to the community

00:19:05.820 --> 00:19:09.180
because there was often
collective punishment for this.

00:19:09.180 --> 00:19:12.390
Behind the Iron Curtain,
you also had the phenomenon

00:19:12.390 --> 00:19:14.160
where a lot of rescuers

00:19:14.160 --> 00:19:15.990
who had often been in the underground

00:19:15.990 --> 00:19:19.710
where the Allies were were persecuted.

00:19:19.710 --> 00:19:22.170
There's also institutional
anti-Semitism there,

00:19:22.170 --> 00:19:23.370
so that was sort of, you know,

00:19:23.370 --> 00:19:26.070
a number of people who
didn't get any recognition

00:19:26.070 --> 00:19:28.500
until after the fall of the Berlin Wall,

00:19:28.500 --> 00:19:30.990
and then, I think the single
biggest reason, though,

00:19:30.990 --> 00:19:33.190
that people were sort of

00:19:34.680 --> 00:19:35.850
tormented after the war

00:19:35.850 --> 00:19:38.373
was they're inconvenient to people.

00:19:39.990 --> 00:19:42.997
They go against the ability to say,

00:19:42.997 --> 00:19:44.268
"I couldn't have done anything"

00:19:44.268 --> 00:19:46.500
because they did do something,

00:19:46.500 --> 00:19:48.630
and they actually, as rare as they were,

00:19:48.630 --> 00:19:51.900
they're sort of an object lesson in that,

00:19:51.900 --> 00:19:54.660
and, you know, Hitler had a secretary.

00:19:54.660 --> 00:19:56.430
After the war,

00:19:56.430 --> 00:19:59.490
she was walking by a
quote from Sophie Scholl,

00:19:59.490 --> 00:20:03.810
and she realized that she
had been the same age as her,

00:20:03.810 --> 00:20:05.670
you know, during the war,

00:20:05.670 --> 00:20:07.080
and when she saw that, she actually said,

00:20:07.080 --> 00:20:09.840
you know, "I actually had
no excuse for what I did,"

00:20:09.840 --> 00:20:11.760
so I think the rescuers,

00:20:11.760 --> 00:20:13.800
people resented very much what they did

00:20:13.800 --> 00:20:16.270
because it kind of put the lie to the idea

00:20:17.751 --> 00:20:20.220
that everyone just had
to go along with it,

00:20:20.220 --> 00:20:22.443
even if you were, you know, against it.

00:20:23.850 --> 00:20:25.680
I'll talk about a couple of, a few,

00:20:25.680 --> 00:20:29.520
just to give you a little,
like, tidbits from the book

00:20:29.520 --> 00:20:32.370
to, hopefully, you'll
wanna read more about it,

00:20:32.370 --> 00:20:34.830
so I'll just talk about
a few different rescuers

00:20:34.830 --> 00:20:35.973
that I cover,

00:20:37.350 --> 00:20:41.043
so this gentleman,
Aristides de Sousa Mendes,

00:20:42.150 --> 00:20:45.210
a review in "The
Christian Science Monitor"

00:20:45.210 --> 00:20:47.370
by a reviewer that I
actually, I don't know,

00:20:47.370 --> 00:20:48.300
but I've been a fan of,

00:20:48.300 --> 00:20:49.640
so I was happy to see him review it.

00:20:49.640 --> 00:20:52.620
He actually called him the
breakout star of the book,

00:20:52.620 --> 00:20:54.720
and when people ask me like,
"Who was your favorite?"

00:20:54.720 --> 00:20:57.600
That's a very hard question,
but he's among my favorites.

00:20:57.600 --> 00:20:59.760
He's very powerful story.

00:20:59.760 --> 00:21:02.970
He's believed to have had
the largest single rescue

00:21:02.970 --> 00:21:05.490
by a single individual
during the Holocaust.

00:21:05.490 --> 00:21:09.750
Some people put the number
as many as 30,000 people.

00:21:09.750 --> 00:21:14.580
He was a aristocratic
aristocrat from Portugal.

00:21:14.580 --> 00:21:15.573
He was a twin.

00:21:16.470 --> 00:21:18.630
His brother was the successful one.

00:21:18.630 --> 00:21:20.310
He became foreign minister.

00:21:20.310 --> 00:21:22.860
Sousa Mendes was one of these diplomats

00:21:22.860 --> 00:21:24.120
that was an amazing entertainer,

00:21:24.120 --> 00:21:26.267
so there were all kinds of amazing people

00:21:26.267 --> 00:21:27.240
always in and out of his house.

00:21:27.240 --> 00:21:30.393
He had a enormous family.
He had 15 children.

00:21:31.650 --> 00:21:32.880
He was a devout Catholic.

00:21:32.880 --> 00:21:33.990
He believed he was actually,

00:21:33.990 --> 00:21:35.970
as many Portuguese are,
a descendant of Jews,

00:21:35.970 --> 00:21:39.000
but, like, hundreds and
hundreds of years ago, earlier,

00:21:39.000 --> 00:21:40.050
and he was a humanitarian,

00:21:40.050 --> 00:21:43.980
and he found himself posted
in Bordeaux in June of 1940

00:21:43.980 --> 00:21:46.020
as the consul general,

00:21:46.020 --> 00:21:48.960
and at that time, as many of you know,

00:21:48.960 --> 00:21:52.530
there was a huge exodus
as the Nazis came in,

00:21:52.530 --> 00:21:54.450
invaded France down from Paris,

00:21:54.450 --> 00:21:56.260
millions and millions of people were in,

00:21:56.260 --> 00:21:59.460
like, the worst traffic
jam in human history,

00:21:59.460 --> 00:22:01.500
and the Luftwaffe was overhead, you know,

00:22:01.500 --> 00:22:03.630
people literally would
see their family members

00:22:03.630 --> 00:22:06.990
killed by machine gun fire,

00:22:06.990 --> 00:22:10.890
and they would go door to door
once they got to the South

00:22:10.890 --> 00:22:12.120
'cause people wanted to get the,

00:22:12.120 --> 00:22:13.710
really, the only way out was through Spain

00:22:13.710 --> 00:22:15.360
and into Portugal and beyond,

00:22:15.360 --> 00:22:17.160
which were two neutral countries,

00:22:17.160 --> 00:22:19.320
and people went door to door to consulates

00:22:19.320 --> 00:22:20.640
just looking for visa.

00:22:20.640 --> 00:22:21.840
You actually need more than one visa,

00:22:21.840 --> 00:22:23.460
but you needed an exit visa,

00:22:23.460 --> 00:22:25.200
and everyone slammed the door,

00:22:25.200 --> 00:22:27.120
including the United States of America,

00:22:27.120 --> 00:22:30.090
and Portugal, too, had Salazar,

00:22:30.090 --> 00:22:31.590
who was the dictator of Portugal,

00:22:31.590 --> 00:22:34.110
was an enormously complicated guy.

00:22:34.110 --> 00:22:37.230
He was sympathetic, at that
point, to the fascists,

00:22:37.230 --> 00:22:39.510
but he really didn't want a refugee crisis

00:22:39.510 --> 00:22:43.110
because he was terrified
of losing neutrality

00:22:43.110 --> 00:22:44.340
and being pulled into the war,

00:22:44.340 --> 00:22:47.310
so he sent out orders

00:22:47.310 --> 00:22:49.807
to all of his diplomats:

00:22:49.807 --> 00:22:54.667
"No refugees are allowed
into Portugal or into Spain,

00:22:54.667 --> 00:22:56.697
"and particularly not Jews."

00:22:57.750 --> 00:23:01.590
Sousa Mendes was in his consulate,

00:23:01.590 --> 00:23:04.290
and there were literally
thousands of people outside,

00:23:04.290 --> 00:23:06.360
tens of thousands of people in the street,

00:23:06.360 --> 00:23:08.130
and he went out one day,

00:23:08.130 --> 00:23:11.670
and he met a rabbi, a young
rabbi who had come from Antwerp,

00:23:11.670 --> 00:23:13.680
and he had been posted in Belgium before,

00:23:13.680 --> 00:23:15.960
and the rabbi had a large family,

00:23:15.960 --> 00:23:17.760
and Sousa Mendes was basically,

00:23:17.760 --> 00:23:19.050
had sent most of his children home.

00:23:19.050 --> 00:23:21.570
He just had his two oldest
sons with him and his nephew,

00:23:21.570 --> 00:23:23.017
and he said to the rabbi,

00:23:23.017 --> 00:23:24.967
"Why don't you come
spend the night in the...

00:23:24.967 --> 00:23:26.520
"Don't sleep on the street;
come to the consulate,"

00:23:26.520 --> 00:23:28.530
and they had this, like,
heart-to-heart conversation,

00:23:28.530 --> 00:23:29.827
and Sousa Mendes said, you know,

00:23:29.827 --> 00:23:32.007
"I can't really issue visas,
but I think I can," you know,

00:23:32.007 --> 00:23:33.480
and he had actually done it

00:23:33.480 --> 00:23:35.760
in violation earlier
already for a couple people,

00:23:35.760 --> 00:23:37.177
and he said, you know,

00:23:37.177 --> 00:23:38.850
"Let me give visas to
you and your family,"

00:23:38.850 --> 00:23:41.977
and the rabbi said to him,
"I can't accept your offer.

00:23:41.977 --> 00:23:43.507
"I can't leave all of my people here.

00:23:43.507 --> 00:23:45.000
"I can't leave everybody on the street,"

00:23:45.000 --> 00:23:47.280
and Sousa Mendes had this very dramatic,

00:23:47.280 --> 00:23:49.890
what he described later
as a nervous breakdown.

00:23:49.890 --> 00:23:51.693
He went into his bed for two days.

00:23:52.680 --> 00:23:53.513
He got up, and he said,

00:23:53.513 --> 00:23:56.787
"I'm gonna give a visa
to anyone who wants it,"

00:23:57.990 --> 00:24:01.500
he said, "regardless of,"
you know, "who you are,"

00:24:01.500 --> 00:24:04.140
and he then issued thousands of visas.

00:24:04.140 --> 00:24:07.200
Each visa could often
accommodate a family,

00:24:07.200 --> 00:24:09.990
and among the people he
saved were Salvador Dalí,

00:24:09.990 --> 00:24:13.170
the entire Habsburg
family, the Rothschilds.

00:24:13.170 --> 00:24:14.003
I opened my book

00:24:14.003 --> 00:24:16.860
with the saving of the
H.A. and Margret Ray,

00:24:16.860 --> 00:24:19.590
who were the authors of "Curious George,"

00:24:19.590 --> 00:24:22.413
who were literally carrying
the manuscript with them,

00:24:25.524 --> 00:24:26.490
and then he was,

00:24:26.490 --> 00:24:28.500
when the door slammed
shut a few weeks later,

00:24:28.500 --> 00:24:30.480
he was summoned back to Lisbon.

00:24:30.480 --> 00:24:32.040
He was fired.

00:24:32.040 --> 00:24:35.940
He had his pension, basically,
dramatically reduced.

00:24:35.940 --> 00:24:38.940
He went through his entire life savings.

00:24:38.940 --> 00:24:41.640
They were literally burning
the furniture to keep warm.

00:24:41.640 --> 00:24:43.560
His wife died prematurely.

00:24:43.560 --> 00:24:46.290
He died, he had a stroke,
and then he died premature,

00:24:46.290 --> 00:24:50.040
like, a relatively young
age, and his life was ruined,

00:24:50.040 --> 00:24:51.930
and I put a couple quotes
from here, you know,

00:24:51.930 --> 00:24:55.320
he survived at one point,
and this was a from,

00:24:55.320 --> 00:24:57.480
he had been from one of the
wealthiest families in Portugal.

00:24:57.480 --> 00:24:58.980
He was going to the Jewish soup kitchen,

00:24:58.980 --> 00:25:03.060
and a teenager working
there remembered later

00:25:03.060 --> 00:25:05.910
he saw him, and he was in his,
you know, diplomatic garb,

00:25:05.910 --> 00:25:06.810
but it was very threadbare,

00:25:06.810 --> 00:25:09.090
and he said to him, he
saw him with his family,

00:25:09.090 --> 00:25:11.130
they had the Jewish part
of the soup kitchen,

00:25:11.130 --> 00:25:13.110
and they had a part for
Portuguese, which was nice.

00:25:13.110 --> 00:25:15.810
He said, "Come to this part,
and you're not a refuge."

00:25:15.810 --> 00:25:18.000
Sousa Mendes says, "We're all refugees,"

00:25:18.000 --> 00:25:20.610
and he had this amazing
line, also, where he said,

00:25:20.610 --> 00:25:22.200
when he made the decision to do this,

00:25:22.200 --> 00:25:24.270
knowing that he was gonna
be in trouble for it later,

00:25:24.270 --> 00:25:27.337
he said, "I would rather
stand with God against man

00:25:27.337 --> 00:25:28.977
"than with man against God,"

00:25:30.264 --> 00:25:33.810
so he's one of my rescuers.

00:25:33.810 --> 00:25:36.600
I tell this story, which
is, like, totally different,

00:25:36.600 --> 00:25:40.050
but I just found it so
fascinating, the circus,

00:25:40.050 --> 00:25:42.780
and this goes to kind of that creativity,

00:25:42.780 --> 00:25:47.160
so the circus is actually a
very multicultural industry.

00:25:47.160 --> 00:25:48.360
It's also a family business,

00:25:48.360 --> 00:25:50.520
so whenever you go to the
circus, they tell you, you know,

00:25:50.520 --> 00:25:53.370
this acrobat troupe was
like the ninth generation,

00:25:53.370 --> 00:25:55.350
and, you know, hundred years ago,

00:25:55.350 --> 00:25:56.940
a lot of the circuses were Jewish,

00:25:56.940 --> 00:25:59.010
and they also were intermarried

00:25:59.010 --> 00:26:01.050
with non-Jewish circus people,

00:26:01.050 --> 00:26:03.180
and there was a family
called the Lorch family

00:26:03.180 --> 00:26:04.890
that invented, you know,

00:26:04.890 --> 00:26:06.270
if you've ever seen someone lean back

00:26:06.270 --> 00:26:09.630
and kind of, like, juggle a person,

00:26:09.630 --> 00:26:11.520
that was their famous act,

00:26:11.520 --> 00:26:13.203
and they were the highest paid.

00:26:14.730 --> 00:26:16.080
You could see that in
the poster on the left,

00:26:16.080 --> 00:26:19.500
the highest single paid attraction.

00:26:19.500 --> 00:26:20.760
They were the star of

00:26:20.760 --> 00:26:24.180
the Ringling Bros. and Barnum
&amp; Bailey Circus in New York,

00:26:24.180 --> 00:26:27.630
and in the '30s, the Nazis
started to, you know,

00:26:27.630 --> 00:26:30.150
basically persecute the Jewish circus,

00:26:30.150 --> 00:26:32.070
and then, eventually
starting to round them up

00:26:32.070 --> 00:26:34.890
and deport them to death camps.

00:26:34.890 --> 00:26:37.380
The girl on the right in the picture

00:26:37.380 --> 00:26:39.300
was an acrobat who was a teenager,

00:26:39.300 --> 00:26:41.370
and she was a member of this family,

00:26:41.370 --> 00:26:44.640
and when things got very scary,

00:26:44.640 --> 00:26:47.250
she approached these two people,

00:26:47.250 --> 00:26:49.680
Adolf Althoff on the
left, and his wife Maria,

00:26:49.680 --> 00:26:51.270
who were circus owners,

00:26:51.270 --> 00:26:53.640
and who, like many circus
owners, were performers,

00:26:53.640 --> 00:26:55.360
and they

00:26:57.030 --> 00:26:58.470
and his siblings did something similar,

00:26:58.470 --> 00:27:00.660
but they hid her in the circus,

00:27:00.660 --> 00:27:02.280
and not only did they hide her,

00:27:02.280 --> 00:27:03.630
she performed in the circus,

00:27:03.630 --> 00:27:06.930
and then they ended up
hiding her entire family.

00:27:06.930 --> 00:27:08.310
She fell in love with a clown,

00:27:08.310 --> 00:27:09.810
and they ended up having
two children during the war,

00:27:09.810 --> 00:27:12.720
so they were rescuing six
people in plain sight,

00:27:12.720 --> 00:27:13.890
and it was a traveling circus,

00:27:13.890 --> 00:27:15.300
and everywhere it went,

00:27:15.300 --> 00:27:18.210
they would have to be
inspected by the Gestapo,

00:27:18.210 --> 00:27:20.280
and Althoff would tell these stories

00:27:20.280 --> 00:27:22.470
about performing in
Russia with dancing bears,

00:27:22.470 --> 00:27:26.250
and somehow they managed
to keep this going

00:27:26.250 --> 00:27:27.550
throughout the entire war,

00:27:29.214 --> 00:27:31.570
and saved this Jewish family

00:27:32.760 --> 00:27:34.173
of performers.

00:27:35.880 --> 00:27:38.880
Very briefly, Greece, you know,

00:27:38.880 --> 00:27:41.430
kind of an unknown story

00:27:41.430 --> 00:27:43.170
in the sense that people know

00:27:43.170 --> 00:27:47.340
that 95% of the Jews
of Greece were killed.

00:27:47.340 --> 00:27:49.560
That was the worst
survival rate in Europe,

00:27:49.560 --> 00:27:51.390
but in the South,

00:27:51.390 --> 00:27:54.480
that was mostly in
Salonica, on Thessaloniki,

00:27:54.480 --> 00:27:55.830
where there was a Sephardic community,

00:27:55.830 --> 00:27:58.290
but in the South there
was a third kind of Jew,

00:27:58.290 --> 00:28:00.870
not an Ashkenazi or a Sephardic,

00:28:00.870 --> 00:28:04.020
but Romaniote, who were there
since Alexander the Great,

00:28:04.020 --> 00:28:08.430
and they actually had a great,
much greater survival rate,

00:28:08.430 --> 00:28:11.310
largely because of the
church and civil society.

00:28:11.310 --> 00:28:12.480
The man in the middle,

00:28:12.480 --> 00:28:15.810
who at one point later
became acting prime minister,

00:28:15.810 --> 00:28:17.460
was the Archbishop of Athens

00:28:17.460 --> 00:28:19.020
and the head of the Greek Orthodox Church.

00:28:19.020 --> 00:28:23.880
He is the only member of a church,

00:28:23.880 --> 00:28:27.000
a head of a church to formally
protest the Holocaust,

00:28:27.000 --> 00:28:28.980
and on the right was the
chief of police of Athens,

00:28:28.980 --> 00:28:30.840
and they led a very large

00:28:30.840 --> 00:28:33.870
and very wide-ranging effort

00:28:33.870 --> 00:28:35.970
to rescue Jews.

00:28:35.970 --> 00:28:37.680
They issued baptismal certificates.

00:28:37.680 --> 00:28:41.430
They issued citizenship
papers under false names,

00:28:41.430 --> 00:28:42.600
and so the Jews of...

00:28:42.600 --> 00:28:45.450
It was also helpful that, you know,

00:28:45.450 --> 00:28:47.970
people, some somewhat unknown,

00:28:47.970 --> 00:28:48.976
that often in

00:28:48.976 --> 00:28:51.810
wherever the Italians
were the occupying force,

00:28:51.810 --> 00:28:53.610
which was in the South,

00:28:53.610 --> 00:28:54.810
they didn't do roundups,

00:28:54.810 --> 00:28:56.610
and the trouble came when the,

00:28:56.610 --> 00:28:59.910
after 1943, when the Italians surrendered,

00:28:59.910 --> 00:29:02.670
and Germans came into the north of Italy.

00:29:02.670 --> 00:29:05.010
That's when the problem,
the roundups started,

00:29:05.010 --> 00:29:07.590
and so the Jews that had made it to Athens

00:29:07.590 --> 00:29:08.940
had the little, you know,
they had a head start,

00:29:08.940 --> 00:29:11.700
and then these people were quite heroic.

00:29:11.700 --> 00:29:13.980
On the left, I will point out,

00:29:13.980 --> 00:29:16.620
there is a book you may
be familiar with right now

00:29:16.620 --> 00:29:19.620
that came out around the same time as mine

00:29:19.620 --> 00:29:22.173
by a prince, Prince Harry.

00:29:24.150 --> 00:29:28.680
I don't know if, you know,
I've read excerpts from it,

00:29:28.680 --> 00:29:31.650
but to me, much more interesting story

00:29:31.650 --> 00:29:34.170
is his great-grandmother,
who's on the left,

00:29:34.170 --> 00:29:35.730
who is Princess Alice.

00:29:35.730 --> 00:29:38.700
She was Prince Philip's mother,

00:29:38.700 --> 00:29:41.730
so she's King Charles's grandmother.

00:29:41.730 --> 00:29:44.700
She was a great-granddaughter
of Queen Victoria,

00:29:44.700 --> 00:29:46.110
which made her a relative

00:29:46.110 --> 00:29:48.270
of pretty much all royalty in Europe.

00:29:48.270 --> 00:29:51.183
Her cousin was the kaiser,
her aunt was the czarina,

00:29:52.380 --> 00:29:55.140
and she, during the war,

00:29:55.140 --> 00:29:59.880
hid a family of Jews,

00:29:59.880 --> 00:30:02.430
well, a mother and two of her children,

00:30:02.430 --> 00:30:07.080
the rest of the brothers had
actually made it to freedom

00:30:07.080 --> 00:30:08.640
and were fighting the Nazis,

00:30:08.640 --> 00:30:12.030
but she hid this family for
several years in her palace

00:30:12.030 --> 00:30:14.130
opposite the Gestapo.

00:30:14.130 --> 00:30:15.510
If they had found her,

00:30:15.510 --> 00:30:17.730
she would've been executed on the spot.

00:30:17.730 --> 00:30:19.890
She was a great hero.

00:30:19.890 --> 00:30:23.160
The story is little known,
even in "The Crown."

00:30:23.160 --> 00:30:25.742
They didn't really talk
very much about it.

00:30:25.742 --> 00:30:28.830
They made her out to be a little
bit of a kook, like, crazy.

00:30:28.830 --> 00:30:30.420
She's actually buried in Israel,

00:30:30.420 --> 00:30:32.280
for different reasons,
not because of this,

00:30:32.280 --> 00:30:34.320
but she's been honored at Yad Vashem,

00:30:34.320 --> 00:30:37.470
and I tell her story in the book,

00:30:37.470 --> 00:30:39.630
and I hope people find it

00:30:39.630 --> 00:30:43.260
at least as interesting as Harry,

00:30:43.260 --> 00:30:46.770
but she was a real heroine,
and a real humanitarian.

00:30:46.770 --> 00:30:49.533
She operated orphanages
throughout the war,

00:30:51.199 --> 00:30:52.599
and was just a great person.

00:30:53.670 --> 00:30:55.350
I talked a little bit about Italy.

00:30:55.350 --> 00:30:57.150
This is another fascinating story.

00:30:57.150 --> 00:30:59.130
Gino Bartali, on the left,

00:30:59.130 --> 00:31:03.720
was one of the most famous
athletes in Europe at the time,

00:31:03.720 --> 00:31:04.860
and particularly in Italy.

00:31:04.860 --> 00:31:07.653
He won the Tour de France in 1938,

00:31:09.392 --> 00:31:11.340
and when he did that, you know,

00:31:11.340 --> 00:31:14.970
sports were externally
important to Mussolini.

00:31:14.970 --> 00:31:18.483
He just liked to show
himself as a great sportsman.

00:31:20.115 --> 00:31:22.290
They had won the World Cup.

00:31:22.290 --> 00:31:26.700
Everybody dedicated their victory to him.

00:31:26.700 --> 00:31:28.770
Bartali refused to do so.

00:31:28.770 --> 00:31:31.770
He was a devout Catholic,
and he did two things.

00:31:31.770 --> 00:31:34.410
He hid the family you see on the right

00:31:34.410 --> 00:31:37.470
in a basement of an apartment
that he owned in Florence,

00:31:37.470 --> 00:31:39.150
and he had some other people as well,

00:31:39.150 --> 00:31:42.150
and then he was part of,
there's a picture of Assisi,

00:31:42.150 --> 00:31:44.550
what was called the Assisi Underground,

00:31:44.550 --> 00:31:48.660
and he would bike with
false identity papers,

00:31:48.660 --> 00:31:49.680
which were lifesaving,

00:31:49.680 --> 00:31:52.080
and because he was so
famous, nobody bothered him,

00:31:52.080 --> 00:31:55.450
and he was also had an excuse
to be biking hundreds of miles

00:31:56.370 --> 00:31:57.780
throughout the north of Italy,

00:31:57.780 --> 00:32:00.540
from Genova to Florence to Rome to Assisi.

00:32:00.540 --> 00:32:02.460
Assisi was sort of the center of this,

00:32:02.460 --> 00:32:04.560
where they were hiding a
lot of Jews and partisans

00:32:04.560 --> 00:32:06.810
in the cloisters, actually,

00:32:06.810 --> 00:32:10.980
and so he, they think he
saved several hundred people

00:32:10.980 --> 00:32:13.440
as part of that underground did,

00:32:13.440 --> 00:32:16.110
and he never told anybody
other than his son.

00:32:16.110 --> 00:32:17.967
He didn't want anybody to know about it,

00:32:17.967 --> 00:32:20.490
and he said, you know, you don't,

00:32:20.490 --> 00:32:21.330
good is something you do,

00:32:21.330 --> 00:32:22.230
and you don't talk about,

00:32:22.230 --> 00:32:23.850
and he said that, you know,

00:32:23.850 --> 00:32:27.420
in life, you win bicycle
races, they give you medals,

00:32:27.420 --> 00:32:28.470
and he said there are other medals

00:32:28.470 --> 00:32:29.490
that are pinned to your soul,

00:32:29.490 --> 00:32:32.010
and you get those in the next life,

00:32:32.010 --> 00:32:34.230
so he was, I mean, just
a fascinating story

00:32:34.230 --> 00:32:37.293
about one of the most famous
athletes in, you know,

00:32:38.340 --> 00:32:40.290
in Europe at the time.

00:32:40.290 --> 00:32:42.243
This is also, you know, interesting.

00:32:43.230 --> 00:32:47.490
I'll go a little bit more quickly.

00:32:47.490 --> 00:32:51.550
On the right is a consul named Sugihara

00:32:52.530 --> 00:32:53.490
and his wife,

00:32:53.490 --> 00:32:57.150
who was a consul from Japan
that was posted in Lithuania

00:32:57.150 --> 00:32:58.290
where there are no Japanese people.

00:32:58.290 --> 00:33:00.270
He was really an intelligence officer,

00:33:00.270 --> 00:33:02.100
and he was there to
monitor troop movements

00:33:02.100 --> 00:33:03.930
because the Japanese were convinced

00:33:03.930 --> 00:33:05.190
the Russians and the Germans

00:33:05.190 --> 00:33:06.420
would go to war with each other,

00:33:06.420 --> 00:33:09.030
and similar to Sousa Mendes,

00:33:09.030 --> 00:33:13.203
he had a lineup all of a sudden
of Jews outside of his home,

00:33:14.400 --> 00:33:15.630
which was also the consulate.

00:33:15.630 --> 00:33:18.930
He ended up issuing
thousands of visas as well,

00:33:18.930 --> 00:33:21.930
and many Jews escaped,
many religious Jews.

00:33:21.930 --> 00:33:24.300
I've had the experience with Sugihara of,

00:33:24.300 --> 00:33:27.210
since I've started talking
and writing about him,

00:33:27.210 --> 00:33:29.160
there are at least six people
I know who are actually,

00:33:29.160 --> 00:33:30.090
that I knew before,

00:33:30.090 --> 00:33:32.160
who I had no idea were
actually rescued by him,

00:33:32.160 --> 00:33:33.090
so he was another...

00:33:33.090 --> 00:33:35.940
Diplomats, in general, had
an opportunity to help.

00:33:35.940 --> 00:33:39.360
Very few of them did, but he
was certainly one of them,

00:33:39.360 --> 00:33:42.540
and again, you know, a
citizen of the world.

00:33:42.540 --> 00:33:44.550
He was fluent in Russian,

00:33:44.550 --> 00:33:49.550
and he was both a Foreign
Service officer and a spy,

00:33:49.950 --> 00:33:50.783
and you can see,

00:33:50.783 --> 00:33:54.780
I mean, he worked 18-hour
days writing visas like this,

00:33:54.780 --> 00:33:58.140
which are almost, you know,
it's like calligraphy,

00:33:58.140 --> 00:34:00.510
and so his hand, I mean,
there's stories of him,

00:34:00.510 --> 00:34:01.980
like, not even being
able to move his hand,

00:34:01.980 --> 00:34:05.040
and he wrote visas until he was recalled,

00:34:05.040 --> 00:34:06.480
and was still writing them

00:34:06.480 --> 00:34:09.270
on the platform of the train station.

00:34:09.270 --> 00:34:11.250
This is Irena Sendler.

00:34:11.250 --> 00:34:13.080
I'm gonna go more quickly

00:34:13.080 --> 00:34:15.300
so we don't just conscious of time,

00:34:15.300 --> 00:34:19.770
but she was 29 years
old social worker 4'11".

00:34:19.770 --> 00:34:21.360
She was part of a network.

00:34:21.360 --> 00:34:25.470
She went in and out of the
Warsaw ghetto almost daily.

00:34:25.470 --> 00:34:28.530
She saved 2,500 small children

00:34:28.530 --> 00:34:30.670
and infants and teenagers

00:34:32.160 --> 00:34:35.640
and was arrested, tortured.

00:34:35.640 --> 00:34:39.870
She survived, but also kept,

00:34:39.870 --> 00:34:41.670
once the ghetto, Warsaw ghetto,

00:34:41.670 --> 00:34:44.670
where there was, like,
enormously inhumane conditions

00:34:44.670 --> 00:34:45.933
was destroyed,

00:34:46.950 --> 00:34:50.310
she then continued to monitor
and follow these children

00:34:50.310 --> 00:34:51.960
until the end of the war,

00:34:51.960 --> 00:34:54.300
and she kept a list of all of their names

00:34:54.300 --> 00:34:55.920
with the hope that maybe their parents,

00:34:55.920 --> 00:34:57.300
who most of whom perished,

00:34:57.300 --> 00:34:58.560
would be able to come back with them,

00:34:58.560 --> 00:35:01.560
and she would bury it under a apple tree

00:35:01.560 --> 00:35:03.690
in her friend's backyard,

00:35:03.690 --> 00:35:07.170
so just an amazingly heroic story

00:35:07.170 --> 00:35:10.173
was completely unknown
until about 10 years ago.

00:35:11.460 --> 00:35:14.820
One thing I just wanted to
touch on is community rescue,

00:35:14.820 --> 00:35:18.030
so some people, many people
are familiar with the Danes.

00:35:18.030 --> 00:35:19.620
You see on the left the king.

00:35:19.620 --> 00:35:21.780
From the king on down,

00:35:21.780 --> 00:35:23.430
there's a apocryphal story

00:35:23.430 --> 00:35:26.700
that the king wore a star of David.

00:35:26.700 --> 00:35:27.840
He didn't because the Danes

00:35:27.840 --> 00:35:31.290
would not permit a Star of
David to be worn by the Jews,

00:35:31.290 --> 00:35:34.620
and the Nazis from the
day of the occupation,

00:35:34.620 --> 00:35:38.310
the Danes refused to any
discrimination against the Jews.

00:35:38.310 --> 00:35:40.350
It's pretty remarkable of the parliament,

00:35:40.350 --> 00:35:41.790
they left the government in place.

00:35:41.790 --> 00:35:45.300
The Fascist Party regularly
got less than 2% of the vote,

00:35:45.300 --> 00:35:47.850
and so the Jews were
left alone for a while,

00:35:47.850 --> 00:35:52.680
and then finally got up to
Hitler, who ordered a roundup.

00:35:52.680 --> 00:35:56.250
A member of the German government there

00:35:56.250 --> 00:35:58.980
actually tipped off the Danish government,

00:35:58.980 --> 00:36:01.833
and the entire, almost the entire country,

00:36:02.700 --> 00:36:06.810
from taxi drivers and medical students

00:36:06.810 --> 00:36:08.553
to I talk about an Olympic rower,

00:36:09.480 --> 00:36:11.823
rowed as kind of like a Dunkirk,

00:36:12.990 --> 00:36:14.490
almost the entire Jewish population,

00:36:14.490 --> 00:36:18.180
7,200 people plus 800 spouses,

00:36:18.180 --> 00:36:19.980
to safety in Sweden,

00:36:19.980 --> 00:36:21.570
and they actually,

00:36:21.570 --> 00:36:25.380
the Nazis caught about 500 who
were sent to Theresienstadt,

00:36:25.380 --> 00:36:27.030
and the government of Denmark

00:36:27.030 --> 00:36:28.650
regularly called to check on them,

00:36:28.650 --> 00:36:30.270
and they were eventually released,

00:36:30.270 --> 00:36:34.620
and so only 50 Danish Jews
didn't survive the war.

00:36:34.620 --> 00:36:35.560
Another place that

00:36:36.690 --> 00:36:39.750
where I talked about
the young man earlier,

00:36:39.750 --> 00:36:41.190
this is Le Chambon,

00:36:41.190 --> 00:36:44.430
which was in the
Protestant area of France.

00:36:44.430 --> 00:36:47.700
They themselves were mostly
descendants of Huguenots,

00:36:47.700 --> 00:36:49.380
and there's a long history

00:36:49.380 --> 00:36:51.040
of being a place of refuge

00:36:53.287 --> 00:36:55.830
from persecution.

00:36:55.830 --> 00:36:57.660
They had already hosted some people

00:36:57.660 --> 00:36:59.610
from the Spanish Civil War.

00:36:59.610 --> 00:37:00.990
It was really a group effort.

00:37:00.990 --> 00:37:02.190
Probably the driving force

00:37:02.190 --> 00:37:05.460
is the couple you see on the left,

00:37:05.460 --> 00:37:09.210
the Pastor Trocmé and his wife,

00:37:09.210 --> 00:37:10.680
who was an Italian aristocrat.

00:37:10.680 --> 00:37:12.450
Trocmé was actually from the North.

00:37:12.450 --> 00:37:13.800
He was from a wealthy family.

00:37:13.800 --> 00:37:14.970
He had spent time in New York.

00:37:14.970 --> 00:37:16.920
He was the tutor to the Rockefellers,

00:37:16.920 --> 00:37:19.170
and then he was sent to
this very poor village,

00:37:19.170 --> 00:37:22.620
and they saved about 5,000 people,

00:37:22.620 --> 00:37:26.760
3,500 Jewish children, many
of whom had lost their parents

00:37:26.760 --> 00:37:29.400
and were taken out of
French internment camps

00:37:29.400 --> 00:37:33.600
in a network with French relief
and the American Quakers,

00:37:33.600 --> 00:37:35.310
and it was pretty amazing.

00:37:35.310 --> 00:37:36.300
I actually had the opportunity

00:37:36.300 --> 00:37:40.830
to talk to the little girl on
the right, who's still around,

00:37:40.830 --> 00:37:42.300
and who is their daughter,

00:37:42.300 --> 00:37:44.370
and was actually a teenager
when this was going on,

00:37:44.370 --> 00:37:47.520
and what you can see here,
there are many pictures

00:37:47.520 --> 00:37:48.810
in the Holocaust Museum and elsewhere,

00:37:48.810 --> 00:37:50.760
is that a lot of the children
that were hidden here,

00:37:50.760 --> 00:37:52.620
because there were more than one school,

00:37:52.620 --> 00:37:54.780
and there were, the villagers,

00:37:54.780 --> 00:37:57.780
they really tried to make
this a normal life for them,

00:37:57.780 --> 00:38:00.690
and even when there would
be, you know, raids,

00:38:00.690 --> 00:38:01.980
they would send them to the forest,

00:38:01.980 --> 00:38:03.390
and say, "We're gonna go pick mushrooms,"

00:38:03.390 --> 00:38:06.570
and the children all felt very safe.

00:38:06.570 --> 00:38:08.340
There was no instance of betrayal,

00:38:08.340 --> 00:38:10.950
and they, most of them talk about,

00:38:10.950 --> 00:38:12.600
I've spoken to some of
them, that they really felt

00:38:12.600 --> 00:38:15.480
like they had a very normal experience.

00:38:15.480 --> 00:38:18.210
They had as close as you could
get in those circumstances

00:38:18.210 --> 00:38:19.860
to, you know, a normal childhood.

00:38:19.860 --> 00:38:22.610
A lot of them, like, viewed
it as being in summer camp,

00:38:23.820 --> 00:38:26.070
and they were, you know, it
was a Protestant village,

00:38:26.070 --> 00:38:27.360
but the Jewish children were,

00:38:27.360 --> 00:38:29.640
they even had, like, a Hanukkah party

00:38:29.640 --> 00:38:31.860
and High Holiday services,

00:38:31.860 --> 00:38:35.280
and there was no attempt
to convert anybody,

00:38:35.280 --> 00:38:39.240
and there was even a
sect there, Darbyists,

00:38:39.240 --> 00:38:42.450
who viewed it as the
Jews as the chosen people

00:38:42.450 --> 00:38:44.550
and were very honored
to be protecting them,

00:38:44.550 --> 00:38:47.610
but you see here, again,
they're, like, normal life.

00:38:47.610 --> 00:38:48.840
I put this in because I know

00:38:48.840 --> 00:38:53.130
there's some experts on Camus.

00:38:53.130 --> 00:38:55.410
He was next door within walking distance

00:38:55.410 --> 00:38:56.310
writing "The Plague,"

00:38:56.310 --> 00:38:58.860
and happy to answer
any questions about it.

00:38:58.860 --> 00:39:01.080
There were always rumors he knew Trocmé.

00:39:01.080 --> 00:39:02.550
I did a little bit of research on this,

00:39:02.550 --> 00:39:04.050
and I can say that there were,

00:39:05.362 --> 00:39:06.870
they had a lot of friends in common,

00:39:06.870 --> 00:39:09.810
and it's pretty clear that
what was going on in Le Chambon

00:39:09.810 --> 00:39:11.667
did have an impact on "The Plague."

00:39:14.010 --> 00:39:16.200
Talked a little bit about the, you know,

00:39:16.200 --> 00:39:17.640
kind of group rescue.

00:39:17.640 --> 00:39:21.030
I'm gonna, you know, just skip
through this a little bit.

00:39:21.030 --> 00:39:24.150
I wrote an article about
this for "The Journal,"

00:39:24.150 --> 00:39:27.480
you know, again, why did
places like Denmark or Albania,

00:39:27.480 --> 00:39:30.810
the only majority Muslim
country in Europe,

00:39:30.810 --> 00:39:32.790
which had more Jews at the end of the war

00:39:32.790 --> 00:39:34.230
than at the beginning, do this?

00:39:34.230 --> 00:39:37.230
And again, it was a number
of things that came together,

00:39:37.230 --> 00:39:38.460
but the most important thing was

00:39:38.460 --> 00:39:41.400
they had no tradition
of religious bigotry.

00:39:41.400 --> 00:39:43.200
They stood together against the Nazis,

00:39:43.200 --> 00:39:46.410
and they often had some ethos, you know,

00:39:46.410 --> 00:39:47.940
whether it was religion.

00:39:47.940 --> 00:39:50.718
Besa is a Albanian culture,

00:39:50.718 --> 00:39:54.810
a ethos of hospitality
that brought them together,

00:39:54.810 --> 00:39:56.500
and one thing I would just say

00:39:57.450 --> 00:40:01.440
is that community matters.

00:40:01.440 --> 00:40:04.800
There was a Dutch rescuer,

00:40:04.800 --> 00:40:08.130
which had a mixed record, but
was better than other places,

00:40:08.130 --> 00:40:10.530
who said, you know, "We, the extreme,"

00:40:10.530 --> 00:40:12.277
like, "the people that were
willing to risk our lives

00:40:12.277 --> 00:40:13.297
"were the tip of the spear,

00:40:13.297 --> 00:40:15.937
"but what really made a
difference was people willing to,

00:40:15.937 --> 00:40:17.197
"our neighbors looked the other way

00:40:17.197 --> 00:40:18.720
"or they were quietly supportive,"

00:40:18.720 --> 00:40:23.190
and so I think that is an
extremely important lesson

00:40:23.190 --> 00:40:25.210
is to have a culture

00:40:26.931 --> 00:40:30.570
that doesn't tolerate,
you know, the bigotry,

00:40:30.570 --> 00:40:32.680
or even kind of

00:40:34.260 --> 00:40:36.180
where people stand up

00:40:36.180 --> 00:40:38.910
when they see somebody being
mistreated and say something,

00:40:38.910 --> 00:40:40.080
and where that's not valued.

00:40:40.080 --> 00:40:42.610
I think who and what we value as a society

00:40:43.500 --> 00:40:46.950
is critical to preventing
anything like this

00:40:46.950 --> 00:40:48.090
ever happening again,

00:40:48.090 --> 00:40:50.040
so happy to kind of, you
know, circle back to that.

00:40:50.040 --> 00:40:51.763
I just, before we go to Q&amp;A,

00:40:51.763 --> 00:40:54.070
I just wanted to do one
Yale thing, which is

00:40:55.110 --> 00:40:58.530
many of you may be
familiar with Varian Fry,

00:40:58.530 --> 00:41:02.340
who was sent to, he was sent
because of this picture,

00:41:02.340 --> 00:41:05.100
which is the very famous
writer, Lion Feuchtwanger,

00:41:05.100 --> 00:41:07.740
who was here behind barbed wire,

00:41:07.740 --> 00:41:09.750
and Eleanor Roosevelt saw this picture,

00:41:09.750 --> 00:41:11.130
and it was one of the catalysts

00:41:11.130 --> 00:41:13.560
that ended up creating the
Emergency Rescue Committee,

00:41:13.560 --> 00:41:15.270
which is now the IRC,

00:41:15.270 --> 00:41:19.020
and they sent Fry over to
try to rescue with a list,

00:41:19.020 --> 00:41:21.150
many of the most famous artists, writers,

00:41:21.150 --> 00:41:24.120
political thinkers in Europe,

00:41:24.120 --> 00:41:27.900
and he did so over 13 months
before he was expelled,

00:41:27.900 --> 00:41:30.660
kind of at the behest
of the State Department,

00:41:30.660 --> 00:41:34.530
and, you know, here he is with
a bunch of the surrealists,

00:41:34.530 --> 00:41:36.400
and he's in the middle in a villa

00:41:37.380 --> 00:41:39.230
that he rented outside of Marseilles,

00:41:40.140 --> 00:41:43.560
and he was part, and so they, you know,

00:41:43.560 --> 00:41:46.920
what a amazing gift and
single-handedly because of him,

00:41:46.920 --> 00:41:48.780
you know, the center of, you know,

00:41:48.780 --> 00:41:51.060
art and literature and culture

00:41:51.060 --> 00:41:52.710
moved from Europe to the United States.

00:41:52.710 --> 00:41:54.660
You can make that argument.

00:41:54.660 --> 00:41:56.700
Here he is with Marc Chagall and his wife

00:41:56.700 --> 00:41:57.990
looking at a painting,

00:41:57.990 --> 00:42:01.380
and I just wanted to point out
that the tall guy in the back

00:42:01.380 --> 00:42:04.950
is a Yalie who was
instrumental to the rescue,

00:42:04.950 --> 00:42:07.350
and his name is Hiram Bingham IV,

00:42:07.350 --> 00:42:10.440
so his father was the Hiram
Bingham III from Machu Picchu.

00:42:10.440 --> 00:42:13.980
He was the governor and
senator from Connecticut.

00:42:13.980 --> 00:42:16.710
He himself was a horrible
racist and anti-Semite,

00:42:16.710 --> 00:42:19.830
which makes, I think, what
Harry did even more remarkable,

00:42:19.830 --> 00:42:22.530
so Harry was the Yale class of '25,

00:42:22.530 --> 00:42:23.760
went to Harvard Law School,

00:42:23.760 --> 00:42:24.810
entered the Foreign Service,

00:42:24.810 --> 00:42:27.570
and was the vice consul in Marseilles

00:42:27.570 --> 00:42:30.180
in 1940 when the Nazis invaded,

00:42:30.180 --> 00:42:34.080
and he was instrumental in helping Fry

00:42:34.080 --> 00:42:37.140
and the entire group of people
around Fry do what they did

00:42:37.140 --> 00:42:40.533
because he issued visas, but
he also hid people in his home.

00:42:41.880 --> 00:42:43.050
There's stories of him

00:42:43.050 --> 00:42:45.603
pulling people out of internment camps.

00:42:47.130 --> 00:42:48.330
He rescued hundreds of people,

00:42:48.330 --> 00:42:50.550
including Chagall and Feuchtwanger,

00:42:50.550 --> 00:42:53.070
but Heinrich Mann, André Breton,

00:42:53.070 --> 00:42:54.693
Max Ernst, Lipchitz,

00:42:57.360 --> 00:43:01.410
like, almost the entire,
you know, surrealist group.

00:43:01.410 --> 00:43:04.500
He also inspected and wrote up reports

00:43:04.500 --> 00:43:07.740
on the conditions in the
French internment camps,

00:43:07.740 --> 00:43:10.470
and he, for his efforts,

00:43:10.470 --> 00:43:12.880
was basically

00:43:14.220 --> 00:43:17.250
transferred to Portugal
and then sent to Argentina

00:43:17.250 --> 00:43:19.560
when he started to tell people
in the State Department,

00:43:19.560 --> 00:43:20.940
you know, there's a lot of, like,

00:43:20.940 --> 00:43:23.910
Nazis and gold flowing into Argentina.

00:43:23.910 --> 00:43:25.020
They moved them to Cuba,

00:43:25.020 --> 00:43:27.180
which was basically a
backwater at the time,

00:43:27.180 --> 00:43:29.310
kind of forced him out
of the Foreign Service,

00:43:29.310 --> 00:43:31.740
and he had a similarly
sad kind of decline,

00:43:31.740 --> 00:43:33.870
although he didn't die, you know,

00:43:33.870 --> 00:43:36.330
early, the way Sousa Mendes
did, but had a sad life,

00:43:36.330 --> 00:43:39.480
and Fry actually, when he wrote his book,

00:43:39.480 --> 00:43:43.237
he inscribed the copy to Bingham,

00:43:43.237 --> 00:43:46.800
"My partner in the crime
of saving human lives,"

00:43:46.800 --> 00:43:49.800
and Fry has been honored.

00:43:49.800 --> 00:43:52.620
I actually think his entire
group should be honored,

00:43:52.620 --> 00:43:55.710
and Bingham was on a U.S. postage stamp,

00:43:55.710 --> 00:43:57.240
and he's received some other awards,

00:43:57.240 --> 00:43:59.343
but he's not been honored by Yad Vashem,

00:44:00.450 --> 00:44:02.950
and he's not been honored
by the State Department.

00:44:04.320 --> 00:44:07.800
Colin Powell gave a citation for the group

00:44:07.800 --> 00:44:12.450
that's for retired State
Department employees,

00:44:12.450 --> 00:44:15.870
but there was a bill entered
into Congress last week

00:44:15.870 --> 00:44:19.020
to honor 60 diplomats with a gold medal,

00:44:19.020 --> 00:44:21.090
and Bingham is on the list.

00:44:21.090 --> 00:44:22.950
He's the only American,

00:44:22.950 --> 00:44:27.180
but a very, you know,
underappreciated figure

00:44:27.180 --> 00:44:29.610
who just, you know, even,

00:44:29.610 --> 00:44:31.740
there are families that
would still, to this day,

00:44:31.740 --> 00:44:33.960
light a candle in his honor,

00:44:33.960 --> 00:44:36.000
Jewish families every
year for what he did,

00:44:36.000 --> 00:44:38.310
and then, if you look at the gift

00:44:38.310 --> 00:44:40.500
that he and Fry and the group around them

00:44:40.500 --> 00:44:42.690
gave to America is pretty remarkable,

00:44:42.690 --> 00:44:45.000
so I couldn't resist,
you know, mentioning him

00:44:45.000 --> 00:44:47.340
because he deserves to be known,

00:44:47.340 --> 00:44:48.300
and (indistinct) more, you know,

00:44:48.300 --> 00:44:50.910
his hundredth anniversary of
his graduation is coming up,

00:44:50.910 --> 00:44:54.243
so, you know, Yale should be proud of him.

00:44:59.310 --> 00:45:03.330
- Thank you. Thank you so
much, Richard, for that.

00:45:03.330 --> 00:45:07.740
Really, you know, great
talk and for the book.

00:45:07.740 --> 00:45:09.970
I really think it's such an important

00:45:10.890 --> 00:45:14.400
and so important to remember these people

00:45:14.400 --> 00:45:15.240
in the way that you do,

00:45:15.240 --> 00:45:17.700
so really moving and interesting,

00:45:17.700 --> 00:45:19.170
so a lot of questions,

00:45:19.170 --> 00:45:22.260
and there are a lot of
questions from the audience,

00:45:22.260 --> 00:45:23.370
so we don't have that much time,

00:45:23.370 --> 00:45:26.640
so we'll just try to do as many as we can.

00:45:26.640 --> 00:45:30.510
First of all, just wondering what kind of,

00:45:30.510 --> 00:45:33.480
how you did your research for this.

00:45:33.480 --> 00:45:35.370
What kind of research went into the book?

00:45:35.370 --> 00:45:38.460
Did you travel around?
Did you interview people?

00:45:38.460 --> 00:45:41.010
Was it mostly secondary research?

00:45:41.010 --> 00:45:43.290
- No, it was, I mean, of course,

00:45:43.290 --> 00:45:44.340
you always do secondary research,

00:45:44.340 --> 00:45:48.690
but I did a lot of primary
research in a lot of archives.

00:45:48.690 --> 00:45:50.670
Yad Vashem is particularly helpful

00:45:50.670 --> 00:45:52.800
because any time anyone gets the award,

00:45:52.800 --> 00:45:54.690
there's, you know, eyewitness testimony

00:45:54.690 --> 00:45:56.940
and a lot of documents that go into it,

00:45:56.940 --> 00:46:00.090
so we did, I think we must have done,

00:46:00.090 --> 00:46:03.150
I say we 'cause I had
some help on translation,

00:46:03.150 --> 00:46:05.310
probably 12 different languages,

00:46:05.310 --> 00:46:07.140
and I also had the opportunity

00:46:07.140 --> 00:46:09.570
to interview a number of survivors,

00:46:09.570 --> 00:46:11.070
most of whom were children,

00:46:11.070 --> 00:46:12.780
and then, I had a fascinating experience

00:46:12.780 --> 00:46:15.333
interviewing a lot of
children of rescuers-

00:46:16.200 --> 00:46:17.820
- Oh, tell us a little bit about that.

00:46:17.820 --> 00:46:20.280
I assume that no rescuers,

00:46:20.280 --> 00:46:21.300
you weren't able to talk to any

00:46:21.300 --> 00:46:22.306
rescuers?
- No, there,

00:46:22.306 --> 00:46:24.033
no, but there was,

00:46:25.560 --> 00:46:29.160
there were, you know,
interviews with a lot of them.

00:46:29.160 --> 00:46:33.090
Actually, I owe Manuscripts
and Archives a thanks

00:46:33.090 --> 00:46:36.450
because during Covid there was
a documentary that was made

00:46:36.450 --> 00:46:39.330
and all the footage is at Yale,

00:46:39.330 --> 00:46:41.100
and they put it up online for me,

00:46:41.100 --> 00:46:42.570
and actually Bingham had a long interview,

00:46:42.570 --> 00:46:44.580
and I ended up sending it to,

00:46:44.580 --> 00:46:45.720
I interviewed three of his children.

00:46:45.720 --> 00:46:47.910
They had not heard their
father's voice since the '80s,

00:46:47.910 --> 00:46:51.150
and so they were, like,
profoundly touched by hearing it.

00:46:51.150 --> 00:46:53.730
I mean, the mix, I found
it really interesting

00:46:53.730 --> 00:46:58.470
because the children,
it's mixed, you know.

00:46:58.470 --> 00:47:01.380
Bingham's children view
him kind of as a saint.

00:47:01.380 --> 00:47:02.730
Fry's son, who I spoke to,

00:47:02.730 --> 00:47:04.350
said his father had a lot of issues,

00:47:04.350 --> 00:47:05.400
which has, I think, come out now,

00:47:05.400 --> 00:47:08.550
and he thought he was bipolar, you know,

00:47:08.550 --> 00:47:11.828
didn't have a close
relationship with him at all.

00:47:11.828 --> 00:47:12.870
He was sort of negative.

00:47:12.870 --> 00:47:15.657
I mean, even the Trocmés'
daughter, you know,

00:47:15.657 --> 00:47:17.550
who had these parents that
were almost, you know,

00:47:17.550 --> 00:47:19.560
they were very prominent
in the nonviolent movement

00:47:19.560 --> 00:47:21.810
when they knew Gandhi, they
knew Martin Luther King,

00:47:21.810 --> 00:47:22.860
they were heroic.

00:47:22.860 --> 00:47:24.513
She said, "Yeah, we were
saving all these Jews,

00:47:24.513 --> 00:47:25.687
"and they were good parents,

00:47:25.687 --> 00:47:27.967
"but like, you know, they
never came to our piano recital

00:47:27.967 --> 00:47:29.010
"'cause they didn't have time,"

00:47:29.010 --> 00:47:30.900
and, you know, like, things like that,

00:47:30.900 --> 00:47:34.320
and, you know, people had
illegitimate children, you know,

00:47:34.320 --> 00:47:37.500
and I think, you know,

00:47:37.500 --> 00:47:40.980
but on the other hand,
it made them seem real.

00:47:40.980 --> 00:47:42.930
I mean, these weren't
people like Mother Teresa

00:47:42.930 --> 00:47:46.050
who set out to be
righteous with their lives.

00:47:46.050 --> 00:47:48.210
They found themselves in this situation,

00:47:48.210 --> 00:47:49.380
and they acted in a certain way,

00:47:49.380 --> 00:47:50.700
and they had flaws, like all of us,

00:47:50.700 --> 00:47:53.220
which I think makes it hopeful.

00:47:53.220 --> 00:47:54.053
- Yeah, exactly.

00:47:54.053 --> 00:47:55.860
I mean, it's so important to show that

00:47:55.860 --> 00:47:57.600
because if everyone is just a saint,

00:47:57.600 --> 00:47:59.607
you think, "Oh, I can never,"
you know, "become like-"

00:47:59.607 --> 00:48:01.800
- No, they were quite the opposite.

00:48:01.800 --> 00:48:04.950
They were multidimensional,
complicated people.

00:48:04.950 --> 00:48:09.900
- So how do you account for
the fact that if, as you said,

00:48:09.900 --> 00:48:13.860
religious belief was one of
the contributing factors,

00:48:13.860 --> 00:48:17.220
that there were so few actual, you know,

00:48:17.220 --> 00:48:19.800
like, how do you account
for the Catholic Church's,

00:48:19.800 --> 00:48:24.800
you know, relative lack of
participation in saving Jews?

00:48:24.870 --> 00:48:28.650
- Yeah, I mean, well, I
think, first of all, you know,

00:48:28.650 --> 00:48:30.360
there's a whole debate
over the pope, right?

00:48:30.360 --> 00:48:34.410
And his predecessor was about
to come out with a statement

00:48:34.410 --> 00:48:37.080
denouncing anti-Semitism in Germany,

00:48:37.080 --> 00:48:38.670
and he died,

00:48:38.670 --> 00:48:41.850
and so I think part of it was at the top.

00:48:41.850 --> 00:48:43.500
I mean, the church is not a monolith,

00:48:43.500 --> 00:48:46.080
so the Bartali story shows that, you know,

00:48:46.080 --> 00:48:47.940
that these part of the church,

00:48:47.940 --> 00:48:49.530
and the main movers there

00:48:49.530 --> 00:48:52.590
were the Archbishop of Florence and Genova

00:48:52.590 --> 00:48:54.660
and the Bishop of Assisi,

00:48:54.660 --> 00:48:59.400
and a lot of the, you know,
the clergy really participated,

00:48:59.400 --> 00:49:02.070
but, again, I think it
has to do with, you know,

00:49:02.070 --> 00:49:05.190
why you belong to a church
or why you, you know,

00:49:05.190 --> 00:49:07.440
so again, people who are
really into hierarchy

00:49:07.440 --> 00:49:08.884
or who are into, I mean,

00:49:08.884 --> 00:49:10.530
the way the church acted was, you know,

00:49:10.530 --> 00:49:12.570
similar to the Foreign Service
in a lot of ways, you know,

00:49:12.570 --> 00:49:16.260
rule-followers and hierarchy,
people attracted to that,

00:49:16.260 --> 00:49:17.760
but people who really internalized

00:49:17.760 --> 00:49:19.860
the teachings of whatever faith,

00:49:19.860 --> 00:49:22.830
and again, they often
got that from a parent

00:49:22.830 --> 00:49:25.230
or maybe from the local
priest they grew up with.

00:49:25.230 --> 00:49:27.081
They tended to be, you know,

00:49:27.081 --> 00:49:29.970
I mean, the people in Le
Chambon were Protestants,

00:49:29.970 --> 00:49:32.010
and they were very devout.

00:49:32.010 --> 00:49:33.270
One of the people who
was there said, you know,

00:49:33.270 --> 00:49:34.680
they didn't read the newspaper every day,

00:49:34.680 --> 00:49:36.180
but they knew their Bible,

00:49:36.180 --> 00:49:37.560
and there was a lot of Bible study,

00:49:37.560 --> 00:49:39.480
so I think if it was a more personal faith

00:49:39.480 --> 00:49:43.350
tended to be more correlated with rescue.

00:49:43.350 --> 00:49:46.590
- Okay, and I'll just encourage
people to submit questions

00:49:46.590 --> 00:49:50.220
using the Q&amp;A function at
the bottom of your screen,

00:49:50.220 --> 00:49:52.927
and I'll take a question
from the audience, which is,

00:49:52.927 --> 00:49:55.150
"Did you find examples

00:49:56.557 --> 00:49:59.017
"of rescuers who cooperated

00:49:59.017 --> 00:50:01.777
"or who formed networks," you know,

00:50:01.777 --> 00:50:04.177
"even," you know, "across Europe?

00:50:04.177 --> 00:50:07.710
"Or was it pretty much,"
like, "an individual thing?"

00:50:07.710 --> 00:50:09.360
- No, it was both,

00:50:09.360 --> 00:50:11.343
so you had individual people,

00:50:12.420 --> 00:50:16.410
like the Althoffs.

00:50:16.410 --> 00:50:20.850
You had countries like Denmark, you know,

00:50:20.850 --> 00:50:24.330
Irena Sendler in Poland
for example, was operating.

00:50:24.330 --> 00:50:25.740
She put together a network of,

00:50:25.740 --> 00:50:28.050
like, Jewish friends in the ghetto

00:50:28.050 --> 00:50:29.670
and non-Jewish friends out of the ghetto,

00:50:29.670 --> 00:50:33.450
and after a while, they merged
with something called Żegota,

00:50:33.450 --> 00:50:36.990
which was a very large
underground organization

00:50:36.990 --> 00:50:39.540
set up to save Jews by two women,

00:50:39.540 --> 00:50:41.370
one of whom was a liberal democrat,

00:50:41.370 --> 00:50:43.500
one of whom was an anti-Semite,

00:50:43.500 --> 00:50:45.090
but who didn't believe you
should exterminate Jews,

00:50:45.090 --> 00:50:48.600
and that was funded by the
Polish government in exile

00:50:48.600 --> 00:50:49.503
and the Allies.

00:50:50.880 --> 00:50:52.410
There were people like Bartali,

00:50:52.410 --> 00:50:55.350
who hid people in his basement by himself,

00:50:55.350 --> 00:50:58.830
but it also was part of
the Assisi Underground,

00:50:58.830 --> 00:51:00.663
so, you know,

00:51:02.790 --> 00:51:03.870
there were, again,

00:51:03.870 --> 00:51:05.967
there were a lot of people
all over Europe who were,

00:51:05.967 --> 00:51:07.470
not a lot, but of the rescuers

00:51:07.470 --> 00:51:09.060
who were people who were
hiding people in their homes.

00:51:09.060 --> 00:51:11.520
I mean, that was, like, the
most common fact pattern,

00:51:11.520 --> 00:51:13.020
but then, there were,

00:51:13.020 --> 00:51:15.240
whenever there was a large-scale rescue,

00:51:15.240 --> 00:51:16.860
often, it was part of a,

00:51:16.860 --> 00:51:19.140
it would part of some
kind of organized network.

00:51:19.140 --> 00:51:20.640
- And, you know, I would,

00:51:20.640 --> 00:51:24.956
someone in the audience
who I know well pointed out

00:51:24.956 --> 00:51:29.130
that Yale's Fortunoff Archive
of Holocaust Testimony

00:51:29.130 --> 00:51:33.720
also has a lot of testimonies of rescuers,

00:51:33.720 --> 00:51:35.430
so not just of survivors,

00:51:35.430 --> 00:51:37.260
and that's an incredible resource.

00:51:37.260 --> 00:51:40.680
Is that something that you
made use of in your research?

00:51:40.680 --> 00:51:43.560
- Yeah, I mean, the video
testimony is really important,

00:51:43.560 --> 00:51:44.430
so I did,

00:51:44.430 --> 00:51:47.430
and then, also the Shoah
project, USC Shoah project.

00:51:47.430 --> 00:51:48.660
I mean, they had a fascinating interview

00:51:48.660 --> 00:51:52.140
with a guy who was a
counterfeiter for Bartali

00:51:52.140 --> 00:51:52.973
who was Jewish,

00:51:52.973 --> 00:51:54.210
and he talked about,

00:51:54.210 --> 00:51:57.180
so those are, yeah,
those are very important,

00:51:57.180 --> 00:51:59.370
and unfortunately, you
know, like, you know,

00:51:59.370 --> 00:52:02.490
as we were talking about, many
people are no longer with us,

00:52:02.490 --> 00:52:04.800
so it's really the only way we can,

00:52:04.800 --> 00:52:05.670
but the first, obviously,

00:52:05.670 --> 00:52:08.670
first-person testimony
is the most important.

00:52:08.670 --> 00:52:11.010
- Mm-hmm, and here,
someone in the audience

00:52:11.010 --> 00:52:14.493
asked what I think is,
you know, the question,

00:52:15.450 --> 00:52:20.167
which is, "Does learning
about these rescuers

00:52:20.167 --> 00:52:23.580
"inspire other people to do the same?"

00:52:23.580 --> 00:52:26.760
It seems like that's where
you're kind of heading,

00:52:26.760 --> 00:52:30.810
but do you have any kind of proof of that?

00:52:30.810 --> 00:52:32.013
Or is it just a hope?

00:52:33.000 --> 00:52:35.730
- Well, it's hard to,

00:52:35.730 --> 00:52:37.627
like, I don't have any
example of someone saying,

00:52:37.627 --> 00:52:40.530
"I did this because," you
know, "Wallenberg inspired me,"

00:52:40.530 --> 00:52:42.750
but I can tell you

00:52:42.750 --> 00:52:46.140
that the outpouring I've gotten from,

00:52:46.140 --> 00:52:49.290
like, a reaction to articles I've written

00:52:49.290 --> 00:52:50.760
or even starting from the book

00:52:50.760 --> 00:52:54.607
would say to me, "Yes, I think most people

00:52:54.607 --> 00:52:57.150
"want to believe that
they would've done this."

00:52:57.150 --> 00:52:59.400
I don't know how many of us would have.

00:52:59.400 --> 00:53:02.400
I mean, it's one thing to risk your career

00:53:02.400 --> 00:53:04.020
or to be inconvenienced.

00:53:04.020 --> 00:53:05.190
It's another thing to risk your life,

00:53:05.190 --> 00:53:06.023
and then, it's another thing

00:53:06.023 --> 00:53:07.800
to risk your, like, children's life,

00:53:07.800 --> 00:53:09.570
which some people in parts of Europe did,

00:53:09.570 --> 00:53:10.770
and that's very extreme,

00:53:10.770 --> 00:53:12.840
and I don't think it's
very hard for us to judge,

00:53:12.840 --> 00:53:13.740
but I do think

00:53:13.740 --> 00:53:17.580
what I hope is that the
inspiration of these people

00:53:17.580 --> 00:53:18.510
would make all of us

00:53:18.510 --> 00:53:22.980
be more like the people that helped them,

00:53:22.980 --> 00:53:27.030
that created the atmosphere
that allowed this to happen

00:53:27.030 --> 00:53:28.440
because that's where you really need

00:53:28.440 --> 00:53:29.550
to have a lot of people,

00:53:29.550 --> 00:53:31.650
but no, I do think people
are very inspired by them,

00:53:31.650 --> 00:53:32.850
and I think, you know,

00:53:32.850 --> 00:53:34.230
we're not living through World War II,

00:53:34.230 --> 00:53:35.730
but there's lots of pain in the world,

00:53:35.730 --> 00:53:37.110
and there's lots that we can do,

00:53:37.110 --> 00:53:39.180
and you can make a difference

00:53:39.180 --> 00:53:42.060
just, again, by standing
up to someone being bullied

00:53:42.060 --> 00:53:43.500
or by helping, you know, support,

00:53:43.500 --> 00:53:46.200
like, you know, things going
on overseas that are horrific

00:53:46.200 --> 00:53:47.033
and speaking out on that,

00:53:47.033 --> 00:53:48.570
and that's all important.

00:53:48.570 --> 00:53:50.910
- Yeah, that kind of leads to a question

00:53:50.910 --> 00:53:53.340
that I had, you know, written down also,

00:53:53.340 --> 00:53:54.780
which is, you know,

00:53:54.780 --> 00:53:57.720
there's so many atrocities going on

00:53:57.720 --> 00:53:59.100
that we hear about all the time

00:53:59.100 --> 00:54:02.460
from, like, you know, child
labor to police killings

00:54:02.460 --> 00:54:06.210
to, you know, what's
going on at the border,

00:54:06.210 --> 00:54:09.030
and, you know, how do you avoid

00:54:09.030 --> 00:54:13.110
just feeling overwhelmed
and paralyzed, you know?

00:54:13.110 --> 00:54:16.320
So you don't have, you're
not actually, you know,

00:54:16.320 --> 00:54:18.180
you think these things are terrible,

00:54:18.180 --> 00:54:19.890
but it's just so overwhelming,

00:54:19.890 --> 00:54:22.140
and you think, like, "What
can," you know, "one person do?"

00:54:22.140 --> 00:54:23.130
So you just do nothing?

00:54:23.130 --> 00:54:25.020
I mean, how do you get past that?

00:54:25.020 --> 00:54:25.853
Do you have any

00:54:25.853 --> 00:54:27.203
insight?
- Yeah, I would say,

00:54:27.203 --> 00:54:29.040
I mean, I think, you know,

00:54:29.040 --> 00:54:31.260
the very famous statement, you know,

00:54:31.260 --> 00:54:34.410
he who saves one life
saves the world, right?

00:54:34.410 --> 00:54:36.750
Is very profound in a lot of ways.

00:54:36.750 --> 00:54:37.770
I mean, one is that, I mean,

00:54:37.770 --> 00:54:40.180
I've experienced that by meeting
descendants of, you know,

00:54:40.180 --> 00:54:42.660
like, there's probably
a million people alive

00:54:42.660 --> 00:54:44.280
because of actions taken in the,

00:54:44.280 --> 00:54:46.140
just the 10 stories in
my book today, right?

00:54:46.140 --> 00:54:47.760
'Cause of just the descendants,

00:54:47.760 --> 00:54:51.570
but also, anything that you
do makes a difference, right?

00:54:51.570 --> 00:54:53.730
So, I mean, in Afghanistan
I know a lot of people

00:54:53.730 --> 00:54:55.170
that were involved with rescuing people

00:54:55.170 --> 00:54:58.053
and just getting one person
out, you know, to that.

00:54:58.920 --> 00:55:01.950
There's a story a friend of mine

00:55:01.950 --> 00:55:03.150
put in his book on Afghanistan

00:55:03.150 --> 00:55:06.150
where, you know, a little
boy is walking along a beach,

00:55:06.150 --> 00:55:08.820
and he sees all these starfish washed up,

00:55:08.820 --> 00:55:12.300
and they're dying because
they need to be in the water,

00:55:12.300 --> 00:55:15.660
and he picks up one, and
he throws it back in,

00:55:15.660 --> 00:55:17.880
and an older man comes over to him

00:55:17.880 --> 00:55:20.580
and says, "Why did you do," like,

00:55:20.580 --> 00:55:22.650
like, "that's not gonna
make a difference,"

00:55:22.650 --> 00:55:24.630
and he says, "Well, made
a difference to that one,"

00:55:24.630 --> 00:55:26.973
so yeah, I mean, the world is,

00:55:27.840 --> 00:55:30.000
and we have much more information
about what's going on now

00:55:30.000 --> 00:55:31.740
on an ongoing basis,

00:55:31.740 --> 00:55:34.890
but I think, again, I've heard, you know,

00:55:34.890 --> 00:55:35.970
Abe Foxman, who's a friend,

00:55:35.970 --> 00:55:37.860
when he spoke at my daughter's school,

00:55:37.860 --> 00:55:41.077
he said, you know, like,
just, again, like, you know,

00:55:41.077 --> 00:55:42.960
"Starting by standing up for somebody,"

00:55:42.960 --> 00:55:43.793
like, I think, you know,

00:55:43.793 --> 00:55:45.270
that anti-bullying campaign is actually,

00:55:45.270 --> 00:55:47.280
people make fun of it, but it's real,

00:55:47.280 --> 00:55:51.720
so, you know, but, you know, we, again,

00:55:51.720 --> 00:55:53.130
supporting people, also,

00:55:53.130 --> 00:55:55.890
who are out there on the
front lines doing something

00:55:55.890 --> 00:55:56.970
is really important

00:55:56.970 --> 00:55:58.230
because not everybody,

00:55:58.230 --> 00:56:00.240
you can't do everything all of the time,

00:56:00.240 --> 00:56:02.460
nor should we expect ourselves to do that,

00:56:02.460 --> 00:56:05.340
but we can all have a big
effect on the culture,

00:56:05.340 --> 00:56:08.010
and again, I would like
to see people today

00:56:08.010 --> 00:56:10.350
who are doing amazing
things much more celebrated.

00:56:10.350 --> 00:56:13.080
I mean, like, I'm not saying
we shouldn't have, you know,

00:56:13.080 --> 00:56:14.670
think like sports people,

00:56:14.670 --> 00:56:16.380
and, like, the Grammys is fun,

00:56:16.380 --> 00:56:18.540
but we should also add into that

00:56:18.540 --> 00:56:21.120
just as much acknowledging
people who are, you know,

00:56:21.120 --> 00:56:23.310
doing things to help people in Ukraine

00:56:23.310 --> 00:56:25.290
or with this earthquake yesterday

00:56:25.290 --> 00:56:27.240
or, you know, other things like that.

00:56:27.240 --> 00:56:28.170
We don't do enough of that.

00:56:28.170 --> 00:56:32.343
That's not who we value as
the media and as a society.

00:56:33.390 --> 00:56:36.630
- Okay, you know, there's
so many other questions

00:56:36.630 --> 00:56:38.700
and people had, like, very specific things

00:56:38.700 --> 00:56:41.220
about, you know, specific rescuers,

00:56:41.220 --> 00:56:45.300
and, you know, unfortunately,
we are out of time.

00:56:45.300 --> 00:56:47.730
I would love to have, you know,

00:56:47.730 --> 00:56:49.530
been able to ask you
all of these questions,

00:56:49.530 --> 00:56:51.870
but I think it's really
a sign that, you know,

00:56:51.870 --> 00:56:54.907
your book is really, you know,

00:56:54.907 --> 00:56:58.570
you know, resonating with
people, and that's great, so-

